\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Australia c.bank says too early to judge impact of market turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wayne Cole   \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2011\par 06:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments, analyst reaction)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Aug 23 (Reuters) - A top Australian central banker on Tuesday said it was still unclear how recent turmoil in world markets would impact on economic activity at home and abroad, suggesting interest rates were on hold in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) Deputy Governor \uc2 Ric Battellino\uc2  said the past year had been a challenging one for policy with a booming mining sector and higher inflation set against subdued consumption and a strong local dollar.\par \par \uc2 Downside risks to the global economy had also increased significantly due to the volatility in financial markets and was a major reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided not to raise interest rates at its monthly policy meeting on Aug. 5.\par \par \uc2 "As you know, since then market volatility has become more extreme," Battellino told an economics conference.\par \par \uc2 "An important issue ahead of us will be to assess what impact this is likely to have on global and domestic economic activity, commodity prices and inflation," he said. "As yet, there is little information on which to base such judgments."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered raising rates this month to head off rising inflation, but decided to stay at 4.75 percent due to acute uncertainty in global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Some investors are pricing in a real chance the global outlook will turn so grim that the RBA will have to reverse course and cut rates in the next few months. The RBA's next board meeting is on Sept. 6.\par \par \uc2 Most economists surveyed by Reuters, however, think the next move will still be up. See\par \par \uc2 "We see no case for lowering the cash rate outside of a return to dysfunctional credit markets," said Roland Randall, senior strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This is not about retail, property, services or manufacturing sectors but a mining investment boom and associated rise in commodities exports to China," he argued. "Hence we still expect the RBA to voice a tightening bias and for interest rates to rise at some point."\par \par \uc2 NO NEED FOR \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  ON A$\par \par \uc2 Answering question on policy after the speech, Battellino emphasised that the local economy was still running near full capacity with unemployment at a low 5.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 He also dismissed suggestions the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to suppress the local dollar, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise had been consistent with \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's booming terms of trade and any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be meaningless.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar edged up to $1.0461 in the wake of the comments, having already been lifted by a surprising improvement in a key barometer of Chinese manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 China is the single largest buyer of Australian resource exports like iron ore and coal, and the major reason the country's terms of trade are at record highs.\par \par \uc2 The RBA was the one of the first major central banks to start tightening back in October 2009, and it lifted rates steadily from 3 percent to 4.75 percent by November last year.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Battellino noted the mining boom had turned out to be even stronger than expected with the terms of trade hitting new highs and forecasts for national income and mining investment revised up over the year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation had also turned out to be higher than expected and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had revised up its forecasts for underlying inflation to 3.25 percent for both 2011 and 2012, above its long-term target band of 2 to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, forecasts for growth in the Australian economy as a whole had been revised down by half a percentage point to 3.25 percent for 2011.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade was due to a combination of weather disruptions to coal shipments, subdued consumer spending, a higher local dollar and a slowing in population growth.\par \par \uc2 "Decisions about monetary policy through the year have sought to balance these various forces," said Battellino. "With the recent volatility in financial markets adding to the uncertainty about the economic outlook, it does not look like the challenge will become any easier over the months ahead."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Wayne Cole; Editing by Mark Bendeich and Ed Davies)\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA-ECONOMY/RBA  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110826e78n0005j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
