\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Offshore Leads Bearish\b0\par\par\uc2 1,787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 01:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index faces broadly negative offshore leads. SPI 200 futures rose 0.1% to 4072.0, the S&P 500 fell 1.5% to 1123.53 and spot gold hit a record high of US$1,880.00 before retreating to US$1,853.40. U.S. 10-year bond yields remained near record lows, at 2.07%. Wall Street's fall was exaggerated by steep falls in \uc2 Hewlett Packard\uc2  and IBM, which accounted for more than half the 1.6% fall in the DJIA. Over the weekend, EU and German leaders hardened their opposition to euro-zone bonds. But on a slight positive note for risk assets, Japan's Nikkei newspaper says Japan was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against yen strength, and China's Xinhua quotes China's president saying China and the U.S. should jointly boost the global economic recovery. S&P 500 Financials fell 1.9%, Materials fell 1.4%, Industrials fell 1.5% and Energy fell 1.7%. Nymex crude oil fell 12 cents to US$82.26. LME copper fell 0.6%. BHP (BHP.AU) ADRs fell to A$37.38 vs A$37.50 locally; the S&P/ASX 200 closed at 4101.9 on Friday. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thailand's economic growth likely decelerated in 2Q as the adverse effect from supply disruptions in the auto industry took its toll. According to the median forecast of seven economists polled by Dow Jones, 2Q GDP likely grew 0.4% on-quarter vs a 2.0% rise in 1Q. On year-on-year basis, 2Q GDP likely rose 3.55% vs a 3.0% rise in 1Q. As Japanese auto makers resumed their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pent-up orders will likely to result in ramped up production in 2H. The clear and present threat for the Thai economy in 2H is the economic hardship in the U.S. and debt problems in the Euro zone. "We need to closely monitor the situation out there in the West. We are hearing more and more bad news on their economy and the material impact on our economy will likely to be felt more in the second half of this year," says Pornthep Jubandhu, a macroeconomic analysis manager at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 . The NESDB is expected to release the 2Q GDP data at 0230 GMT Monday. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] Even as the EUR/USD pares some of its morning declines in recent action, traders still expect the pair to feel the brunt of rampant economic concern about the region. David Scutt, treasury dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Arab Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says if Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  occurs, that could further propel a slide. "The U.S. dollar should be modestly bid throughout, but a lot depends on whether the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets again. If they get involved, expect the euro to come under renewed pressure," says Scutt. The EUR/USD is at 1.4374, down from 1.4394 late Friday but up from a morning low of 1.4361. Scutt tips support at 1.4340, then 1.4270. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2237 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices likely will be well supported Monday after U.S. grain futures jumped Friday on increasing concerns about crop losses due to poor weather in the Midwest and northern Plains earlier this year. CBOT soft red winter wheat for December delivery jumped 22.0 cents, or 3%, to US$7.6125/bushel. The AUD/USD is trading around 1.0366, little changed from Friday morning but down about a cent from early Saturday. On Friday, the most active ASX wheat futures deliverable January next year in New South Wales settled unchanged at A$258.50/metric ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD is trending lower in early Asian action, in part moved by momentum from the Libyan rebels against one of Col. Moammar Gadhafi's elite military units, says Christopher Gore, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader and analyst at Go Markets. The EUR/USD is at 1.4367, down from 1.4394 late Friday. "If we see Gadhafi unseated, we would see a relief rally in the price of oil, and with the U.S. dollar having an inverse relationship to crude, that would promise weakness across oil and a U.S. dollar bounce," says Gore, who promotes buying against commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the CAD and the AUD. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2159 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is likely to open down as the weak night on Wall Street Friday will weigh on sentiment although investors will also be digesting corporate results from companies like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Contact Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CEN.NZ), Cavalier (CAV.NZ) and Nuplex (NPX.NZ), says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Adrian Vance. "It will be a fairly slow, subdued start and people will be fairly tentative," he says. "No one is going to be too bullish to begin with... but depending on what happens with some of these other results, we will probably stabilize quite quickly," he adds. He says there could be some profit taking in Telecom (TEL.NZ), which gained Friday after a positive FY11 result. The NZX-50 ended down 0.6% at 3267.84 Friday. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2122 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is at 0.8189 early in New Zealand after tumbling overnight Friday and is likely to trade sideways in Asia, says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Burrowes. "The market will spend the week positioning for Bernanke's speech at Jackson Hole on Friday night," he says. He notes, however, there will be some interest in domestic data, such as the Reserve Bank of New Zealand's survey of inflation expectations Monday and merchandise trade and retail trade figures later in the week. He says support is currently at 0.8130 while resistance is at 0.8245. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2056 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 76.75 versus 76.54 late Friday in New York, but "as we start this week, everything is going to be fairly tentative as we go into Jackson Hole," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Senior Trader Stuart Ive. The pair fell as low as 75.94 Friday in New York after Takehiko Nakao, Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs, said Japan would take a measured approach to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, but then pared most of its losses. Ive says if the BOJ were to opt for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it would be unlikely to take place before the Jackson Hole meeting. He also notes that, in general, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are trading in increasingly wider ranges. "Markets are illiquid, and flows are dictating moves," he says, adding that markets remain extremely jittery. He says support is around 75.90 while resistance is at 77.30. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2018 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: Unconvinced by the near constant risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders sent the USD to a new record low against the yen Friday. The dollar fell as low as Y75.94 before clawing back most of those losses. The USD also weakened against the CHF, though the possible threat of action from its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also looms over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Earlier Friday, Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy bureaucrat said that fundamentals weren't driving demand for the yen, an oft-repeated piece of rhetoric. There's an element of speculation behind the trading, said Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs. Switzerland is facing an eerily similar fate as investors pour into the franc and out of the dollar and euro. The EUR/USD was at 1.4396 late Friday vs 1.4334 late Thursday, the USD/JPY was at 76.54 vs 76.57, the EUR/JPY was at 110.20 vs 109.75, the GBP/USD was at 1.6479 vs 1.6513 and the USD/CHF was at 0.7848 vs 0.7941. The ICE Dollar Index was at about 73.990 from about 74.244. Stocks closed down as investors continued worrying about a potential global recession and the health of the European banking system. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . dropped 20% as investors fretted about the world's largest personal-computer maker's extensive plans to reshape its business model. Fellow blue chip International Business Machines Corp. fell 3.8%. Both the Dow and the Nasdaq fell 1.6%. The 30-year bond's price rose for a fourth straight session and was Friday's best performer. In contrast, many shorter-dated notes traded flat to slightly weaker, a day after the rally that sent benchmark yields to historic lows; the five-year yield rose 0.7 bp to 0.900%, while the 10-year yield was down 1.4 bps at 2.069%. Crude futures settled nearly flat, pausing after steep drops earlier this week as traders weigh the threat of an economic slowdown against persistent oil demand. September Nymex crude settled 12 cents lower at $82.26/bbl. December Comex gold settled $30.20 higher at a record $1,852.20 a troy ounce. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1952 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is at 0.8179 early Monday versus 0.8222 late Friday and is likely to head lower to start off the week as Asian equity markets react to Friday's European and U.S. market moves, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton. He says any moves toward 0.8100 will be cushioned only by support from local exporters. Sinton says support should hold at 0.8090, while resistance is at 0.8220. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 21, 2011 19:01 ET (23:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110821e78l003e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
