\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Australian Dollar Under Pressure, Pares Some Declines\b0\par\par\uc2 By Geoffrey Rogow   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2011\par 03:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar rose slightly from its early Wednesday low in recent Asia trade but still faced heavy pressure as the devastating impact of the floods ravaging the state of Queensland continued to weigh on sentiment about the country.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar recently changed hands at US$0.9853, up from a morning low of US$0.9806 that had served as its lowest level since Dec. 9.\par \par \uc2 The increasing impact of floods that began in December and have dented local economic forecasts and confidence have weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for much of the past week. After surging broadly in the second half of 2010 on the back of a mining boom and a string of interest rate hikes from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , concerns about the floods' impact on the area's mining and farming industries have coupled with broader European debt concerns to weigh on the Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 Those concerns were further highlighted in morning trade by a string of downbeat forecasts from local economists. Of the more than a dozen economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires, market experts now estimate the flooding will shave around 0.30-0.75 percentage points off Australia's gross domestic product growth, spread over the final quarter of last year and the first three months of 2011.\par \par \uc2 While some of the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decline early Wednesday in Asia was pared by better-than-expected Australian housing finance data at 0030 GMT, traders noted the risk remains of further weakness on future flood updates and heavily-anticipated labor force data due on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to react on the headlines on the Queensland issues until we get jobs data tomorrow. Technically, though, the Aussie is soft and the risk is a little bit lower," said Philip Burke, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief dealer for \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Burke pointed to support around the morning low of US$0.9806, with resistance around US$0.9870.\par \par \uc2 Weeks of heavy rains and flooding have already taken a heavy economic toll on Queensland and its booming coal mining industry, with roughly 10 billion Australian dollars (US$9.88 billion) in clean-up and lost revenue forecast by government leaders. Early Wednesday, Australian authorities warned the death toll from the floods, which currently stands at 10, could more than double as rescue efforts continue. Thousands of Australians have been forced to evacuate, with waters now threatening the state's capital of Brisbane.\par \par \uc2 The push on the Australian dollar, the fifth most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, is a stark contrast to 2010 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the year at US$1.0231 after reaching parity with the U.S. dollar for the first time since it was floated in 1983.\par \par \uc2 Even with the 4% drop in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start 2011 and the dire economic forecasts, traders weren't too concerned about the long-term impact of the floods on the Australian dollar, with RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Greg Gibbs saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should rebound as soon as the flood waters peak. China's insatiable demand for Australia's iron ore and coal should help pay for reconstruction, with likely higher inflation from the floods forcing the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates -- driving yield-thirsty investors into Australian dollars.\par \par \uc2 But given the big drop happened so quickly, reversing months of buying, some economists in the country have said more declines could force the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an argument that has been dismissed by those in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "The (RBA) only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth liquidity and while the market's been down, it's been pretty smooth," said a senior Sydney-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.\par \par \uc2 The RBA declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 While the Australian dollar sank, bonds were slightly lower after a Tuesday surge. Traders had pushed out pricing of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next rate hike to August and even pricing in a 24% chance of a rate cut in February on the floods in recent days, with some viewing the move as overdone and taking the opportunity to sell.\par \par \uc2 "It's certainly a good time to lock in some cheap short-term funding; currently pricing in a 2% chance of a rate hike by mid-year, which looks pretty rich to me," said Adam Carr, senior economist with ICAP in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Geoffrey Rogow, Dow Jones Newswires; +61-2-8272-4686; geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-01-11 0200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110112e71c0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
