\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Australia's Swan: Artificially Lowering A$ Value Would Be Dangerous\b0\par\par\uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2010\par 06:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 18 Oct 2010 00:10 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Swan: Consequences Of A$ \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Would Be Higher Inflation, Rates\par \par \uc2 18 Oct 2010 00:10 EDT *DJ  Australia's Swan: Global Confidence In Economic Management Would Collapse\par \par \uc2 18 Oct 2010 00:10 EDT DJ  Australia's Swan Says Artificially Lowering A$ Value Would Be Dangerous\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan said artificially lowering the value of the Australian dollar would be dangerous, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached parity with the U.S. dollar on Friday for the first time since it was floated in 1983.\par \par \uc2 "The consequence of (artificially lowering the value of the Australian dollar) would of course be higher inflation and then higher interest rates, and with it a global collapse of confidence in the management of the Australian economy," Swan said in parliament on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's dangerous," he added.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Australia's ruling minority Labor government has come under pressure from the opposition Liberal-National coalition to address the rising dollar and its impact on exporters. The coalition has called for an immediate economic statement with new policy measures designed to take upward pressure off interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Swan has given his starkest rejection of those calls yet, saying the move would even hurt some of those most calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --namely the local manufacturing, agriculture and tourism industries, as well as homeowners. In addition, any action to artificially lower the value of the Australian dollar would encourage retaliation from the countries' trading partners, he said.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, Swan said any move to lower the Australian dollar's value would risk "fracturing the long-held bipartisan consensus on the floating exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 The comments are in step with \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which decides whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said recently it would be a mistake to try to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A swooning U.S. dollar has pushed Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up sharply in recent months, prompting several Asian countries, including Japan, South Korea and Indonesia, to sell their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to protect their export industries.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar jumped to US$1.0003 on Friday after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke strongly suggested more quantitative easing measures would be announced in November to bolster flagging activity in the world's biggest economy. But the U.S. dollar later rebounded, and Australian dollar was trading at US$0.9849 early Monday afternoon in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Australian exporters have reported strain from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, which has stemmed from weakness in the U.S. dollar and expectations the RBA will raise interest rates further in coming months. The RBA has held off on rate increases since May, but it led the world's central banks in tightening monetary with 150 basis points of rate rises starting in October 2009.\par \par \uc2 The RBA's official cash rate target currently stands at 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 The last time the RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  it acted to support the Australian dollar in the period surrounding the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008.\par \par \uc2 -By Geoffrey Rogow, Dow Jones Newswires; +61-2-8272-4686; geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (James Glynn contributed to this report.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 18, 2010 00:10 ET (04:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101018e6ai000jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
