\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Australian dollar hits parity with US dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 15:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with detail throughout)\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 15 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar rose above parity against its U.S. counterpart on Friday for the first time since becoming a freely-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , extending a bull run that appears well-entrenched because of Australia's higher rates and robust, resource-driven growth.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar <AUD=D4> rose as high as $1.0004, finally taking out the round-number, psychological level that analysts and market players have been eyeing for months.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that the Federal Reserve is on the verge of pumping more money into the U.S. economy in a process known as quantitative easing has also buoyed the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Remarks from Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke on Friday gave it a final push above the $1 parity level. [ID:nNLAFLE62N].\par \par \uc2 The Aussie has been the strongest major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Australia skirted through the global financial crisis without falling into recession.\par \par \uc2 In fact, its economy picked up steam, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known at home as the "little battler" for the pounding it took after it was freely floated in 1983, has surged 66 percent since touching a low of $0.6007 in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated after rising above parity and was last trading at $0.9920, and analysts warn trading will not be a one-way bet, with the latest data showing speculators already heavily long the Australian dollar. [IMM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ]\par \par \uc2 But traders said the pull-back was mostly driven by profit-taking, with support seen around $0.9908 and $0.9825, which is on a short-term trend line.\par \par \uc2 Charts suggest the Aussie could rise as high as $1.0236 in coming weeks, the 161.8 percent Fibonacci projection level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall between November 2009 and May 2010.\par \par \uc2 REASON TO KEEP BUYING\par \par \uc2 Unlike other countries griping about excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength against a sliding U.S. dollar, Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has considered a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a natural outcome of the country's booming trade in natural resources and a tool for fighting inflation.\par \par \uc2 Australia's record trade boom could last for years, as the country sells most of the commodities needed by rapidly urbanizing China and India, including iron ore, coal, uranium, gas, wheat and cotton.\par \par \uc2 About 60 percent of Australia's sales abroad are commodity exports, and top trading partner, China, sucks in about a fifth of all total exports. Proximity to China and India has given Australia a leg up over resource-rich Canada, which exports primarily to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Surging demand from emerging Asia means Australia's export earnings from food, minerals and energy are expected to reach a record A$215 billion in the year to June 2011, a jump of 26 percent from the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Head-turning profits have in turn led to an investment binge. Analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  estimate that total resource projects underway, planned or under consideration, amount to A$725 billion, or nearly 60 percent of Australia's annual gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 That is fast-depleting spare capacity in the economy and has put the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) on inflation watch. And with low rates in developed countries driving capital into faster-growing emerging economies, there's little risk of the RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 <------------------------------------------------------\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade/inflation chart:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/kyj48p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/kyj48p}}}\uc2 ? ANALYSIS-Australia inflation riskier than debt [ID:nSGE68R00P] ANALYSIS-Canada dlr rally could stall soon [ID:nN13265832]\par \par \uc2 ------------------------------------------------------>\par \par \uc2 The RBA has said rates need to rise from the present 4.5 percent, which is well above the Group of 7 rich nations average of 0.55 percent. That increases its appeal over lower-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has already driven U.S. rates to near zero and is widely expected to pump more money into the economy in November, pushing the greenback down further. A falling U.S. dollar also drives up commodity prices, benefiting Australia.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring that, spreads between two- and 10-year Australian and U.S. yields, which historically are highly correlated to the Australian dollar, are near their widest in more than two years. <0#AUBMK=> <0#USBMK=> (Reporting by Reuters bureaus worldwide; Editing by Padraic Cassidy)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /AUSSIE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101015e6af0011z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
