\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Likely Firm; May Hit 4720\b0\par\par\uc2 2,119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 00:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to follow bullish lead of Wall Street, commodity prices and Australian dollar after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data Friday fuelled expectations of U.S. quantitative easing when Fed meets Nov. 2/3. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise in S&P/ASX 200 to 4699, with materials and energy sectors expected to lead gains on offshore peer strength. On the charts, a sustained break of major resistance at 4700 (61.8% retracement of April-May fall) could trigger a rise to the April peak at 5025, which is also the inverse head & shoulders pattern target, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. The bulk of any rise to 5025 may occur before the next FOMC meeting, after which time a "sell-on fact" reaction to any quantitative easing is possible. In the short-term, there's some chance of a bearish 5-point reversal pattern forming, with resistance from that pattern projected at 4720, which is also upper Bollinger band resistance. If the index makes a new 5-month high above 4698 today but closes below that level, profit-taking could see last week's low at 4559.0 tested. A break of that level would trigger the 5-point reversal, which could generate a more significant drop. That could occur if offshore investors take profit as AUD/USD rises toward parity. But it's premature to predict such a move just yet. Index last 4681.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,353.85/oz, up $6.90 early today, driven by weaker USD. ScotiaMocatta's chart analysis shows gold last week posted its ninth up-week in past 10 weeks, in a move that saw it run to $1,365 from $1,157. "There are no reversal signals on the weekly chart so the bias remains for another push higher," with $1,320, $1,312 as important supports, bank says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Queensland government to test investor appetite for IPOs by offering 1.464 billion-1.684 billion QR National shares at A$2.50-A$3.00, giving company a market value of A$6.1 billion-A$7.32 billion, with an expected additional A$500 million of drawn debt. QR National has released ambitious targets, forecasting EBITDA to grow to A$894 million this FY, from A$628 million in FY10, and to A$1.10 billion in FY12, although dividends will be a more modest 3.7c/share in FY11, 8.3c in FY12. The rail haulage company is pinning its growth prospects on sustained demand for coal from fast-developing Asian nations like China. FY12 earnings forecast gives company enterprise value-to-EBITDA ratio of 6.0-7.1 times, which is a discount to valuation of competitor Asciano (AIO.AU), which, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 , is trading at FY12 EV/EBITDA ratio of 7.4 times. That discount, plus incentives to retail investors, who will pay no more than A$2.80 a share and receive 10 cent discount to price paid by institutional investors, may help ease concerns float will struggle to overcome recent aversion to IPOs, with department store Myer (MYR.AU) losing value immediately after floating in November and termination of construction company Valemus float in July. Last biggest company to float on Australia's stock exchange was \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) and its shares hit a record low last week. (ross.kelly@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2220 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4717.0 after weak U.S. jobs data fuelled expectations of quantitative easing in the U.S. DJIA up 0.5% at 11006.48, S&P 500 up 0.6% at 1165.15, Nasdaq up 0.8% at 2401.91. Over the weekend, IMF/G7 meetings failed to resolve conflict over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Fortescue Metals\uc2  signed a US$2.04 billion corporate bank facility. On Friday, U.S. September nonfarm payrolls fell 95,000 vs 10,000 fall expected, and private sector jobs rose just 64,000 while jobless rate remained at 9.6%. S&P 500 Financials up 0.1%, with KBW Bank index down 0.2%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  fell 1.0% after placing a moratorium on foreclosures. S&P 500 Materials up 2.0%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 5.7% on strong 3Q revenue and improved 2010 alumina demand forecast. Freeport up 4.5%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  up 3.3%, Barrick Gold up 1.9%. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$41.41 vs local close at A$41.00. London-listed BHP up 1.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.8%. S&P 500 Industrials up 0.8%, with Caterpillar up 2.1%. S&P 500 Energy up 1.1%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 1.1%, with \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  up 1.8%. Spot gold at US$1,346.85 vs US$1,333.00. Nymex crude oil up 1.2% at US$82.66. LME copper up 2.6%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 4.0%, nickel up 2.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3992 vs 1.3926 this time Friday, AUD/USD 0.9894 vs 0.9826. S&P/ASX 200 last 4681.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2219 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely lifted by Wall Street gains Friday; worse-than-expected payrolls data heightened expectations of more quantitative easing from Fed, cheering market. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, investors on guard for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities after USD/JPY touches new 15-year low in early Asia, though Japan markets shut for Health-Sports Day, should curtail trading volumes in Asian session, possibly make for more volatility. USD/JPY at 81.46 vs 82.08 in late NY trade Friday; EUR/JPY at 114.40 vs 114.33, EUR/USD at vs 1.3929 vs 1.3987. On data slate: Australia housing finance at 0030 GMT, Malaysia manufacturing sales, industrial production at 0400 GMT. Later there's France industrial production, Italy industrial production, UK RICS housing market survey, BRC-KPMG retail sales monitor, New York Fed president Dudley speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2214 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan points to downside of rising AUD/USD, saying it could affect budget revenues as company profitability is hit. Government has plans to restore surpluses by 2012-13. Swan tells weekened television weakened tourism and manufacturing sectors would also be addressed by government policy. Comments are a reflection of rising official concerns about pair, which is now 20% above June lows, having reached 27-year highs last week. Concern has yet to become jawboning and certainly there's no hint RBA will come off the sidelines to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it is evidence that high exchange rate will have direct impact on policy decisions. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2211 GMT [Dow Jones] USD retreating broadly in early Asia trade, with EUR/USD breaking through 1.400 and USD/JPY falling to fresh 15-year low. "The market is saying this employment report on Friday wasn't enough to keep Fed from quantitative easing; the trend is still lower bond yields and a weak dollar," says Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As USD/JPY pushes to low of 81.40, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk has already ratcheted up on trading desks in early trading, says Grace. "We may see BOJ anytime in the market with dollar-yen falling. If BOJ comes into the market, that would get a dollar bid in the market." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2200 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: DJIA closed above 11000 Friday for first time since May as investors hope weak jobs report increases odds Fed will take new steps to jump-start economy at its next meeting on Nov. 3 by purchasing more Treasurys. Market seems "completely focused on QE2, so negative news is good because it makes it more likely the Fed will do it," says John Apruzzese, chief investment officer of Evercore Wealth Management. "Looking longer term, the QE is not a panacea--if it doesn't work, then we're going to be in serious trouble." \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  gained 5.7% after kicking off earnings season late Thursday; results exceeded analysts' expectations and company lifted forecast for global aluminum demand in 2010. Caterpillar +2.1%, \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  +1.8% and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  gained +1.7%. Dow +0.5%, Nasdaq +0.8%, Philly semicons +1.0%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2148 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices likely to jump today after U.S. wheat futures surged to a daily exchange-imposed limit Friday as a shockingly low forecast for U.S. corn output re-ignited fears about tightening grain supplies. Soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT settled up 60 cents, or 9%, at a 2-week high of US$7.1925/bushel. Corn for December delivery also went limit-up. Corn and wheat linked because both used for animal feed. Demand for wheat could increase with less corn available. A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit gain for Australian wheat today; AUD/USD last 0.9894, up from 0.9720 late Friday. On Friday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales rose A$4 to A$254/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia up A$5.00 at A$290.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2139 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Expectations U.S. Fed will take measures to stimulate economy helped lift base and precious metals Friday; LME 3-month copper surged $210 to $8,305/metric ton, tin $845 to $26,345, nickel $475 to $23,375, lead $64 to $2,272, zinc $29 to $2,290, and aluminum $95 to $2,420. Gold futures on Comex in NY jumped Friday after bleak U.S. employment figures strenthened expectations of further U.S. monetary easing, sending investors into the metal to hedge a weakening USD. "The gold market is interpreting it as keeping the spigot on for liquidity," says Jim Steel, senior vice president and metals analyst with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in NY. Spot gold $1,344.90/oz, down $1.95 early today from late Friday, when it bounced to an intraday high of $1,349.75 from a low of $1,324.95. Some other nations also undertaking monetary easing measures in a bid to support their exporters has also led to buying gold as a broader alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Everyone's trying to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; no-one's sure who's going to win," says Craig Ross, vice president of Chicago-based brokerage ApexFutures.com. "So do you want to be in paper, or in a metal that at least isn't going to hurt you no matter who wins?" Of other precious metals, silver slumped 72 cents Friday to $23.22/oz, though platinum up $6 at $1,701 and palladium $1 at $584. [ 10-10-10 1852ET ]\par \par \uc2 Nymex November crude futures down 99 cents on Friday at $82.84. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2126 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government bonds will be supported in Asia session says Tony Morriss, debt strategist at \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . Says weekend's meeting of IMF/G7 failed to add anything new for markets, so focus remains on U.S. looming QE measures. Adds heavy bond supply from U.S. this week could mute gains somewhat. December 3-year bonds now 95.08, unchanged. Range for session likely 95.04-95.15, Morriss says. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2109 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD supported in early trading but unlikely to extend too much higher for now. Grant Turley, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ, says IMF/G7 meetings over the weekend failed to resolve any of the lingering uncertainties in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so it's "business as usual", with USD weakness likely to persist amid ongoing chatter of forthcoming QE measures from the Fed. AUD/USD could extend to post-float highs of 0.9918, but any thrust toward parity likely to be left for European and U.S. session. Holiday in Japan likey to quieten Asia session. AUD/USD now 0.9893. Support at 0.9850, where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exporter demand is likely. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 10-10-10 1852ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101010e6aa0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
