\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: QR National Valuation At Discount To Asciano\b0\par\par\uc2 2,200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 00:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Queensland government to test investor appetite for IPOs by offering 1.464 billion-1.684 billion QR National shares at A$2.50-A$3.00, giving company a market value of A$6.1 billion-A$7.32 billion, with an expected additional A$500 million of drawn debt. QR National has released ambitious targets, forecasting EBITDA to grow to A$894 million this FY, from A$628 million in FY10, and to A$1.10 billion in FY12, although dividends will be a more modest 3.7c/share in FY11, 8.3c in FY12. The rail haulage company is pinning its growth prospects on sustained demand for coal from fast-developing Asian nations like China. FY12 earnings forecast gives company enterprise value-to-EBITDA ratio of 6.0-7.1 times, which is a discount to valuation of competitor Asciano (AIO.AU), which, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 , is trading at FY12 EV/EBITDA ratio of 7.4 times. That discount, plus incentives to retail investors, who will pay no more than A$2.80 a share and receive 10 cent discount to price paid by institutional investors, may help ease concerns float will struggle to overcome recent aversion to IPOs, with department store Myer (MYR.AU) losing value immediately after floating in November and termination of construction company Valemus float in July. Last biggest company to float on Australia's stock exchange was \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) and its shares hit a record low last week. (ross.kelly@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2220 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4717.0 after weak U.S. jobs data fuelled expectations of quantitative easing in the U.S. DJIA up 0.5% at 11006.48, S&P 500 up 0.6% at 1165.15, Nasdaq up 0.8% at 2401.91. Over the weekend, IMF/G7 meetings failed to resolve conflict over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Fortescue Metals\uc2  signed a US$2.04 billion corporate bank facility. On Friday, U.S. September nonfarm payrolls fell 95,000 vs 10,000 fall expected, and private sector jobs rose just 64,000 while jobless rate remained at 9.6%. S&P 500 Financials up 0.1%, with KBW Bank index down 0.2%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  fell 1.0% after placing a moratorium on foreclosures. S&P 500 Materials up 2.0%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 5.7% on strong 3Q revenue and improved 2010 alumina demand forecast. Freeport up 4.5%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  up 3.3%, Barrick Gold up 1.9%. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$41.41 vs local close at A$41.00. London-listed BHP up 1.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.8%. S&P 500 Industrials up 0.8%, with Caterpillar up 2.1%. S&P 500 Energy up 1.1%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 1.1%, with \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  up 1.8%. Spot gold at US$1,346.85 vs US$1,333.00. Nymex crude oil up 1.2% at US$82.66. LME copper up 2.6%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 4.0%, nickel up 2.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3992 vs 1.3926 this time Friday, AUD/USD 0.9894 vs 0.9826. S&P/ASX 200 last 4681.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2219 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely lifted by Wall Street gains Friday; worse-than-expected payrolls data heightened expectations of more quantitative easing from Fed, cheering market. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, investors on guard for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities after USD/JPY touches new 15-year low in early Asia, though Japan markets shut for Health-Sports Day, should curtail trading volumes in Asian session, possibly make for more volati