nth copper surged $210 to $8,305/metric ton, tin $845 to $26,345, nickel $475 to $23,375, lead $64 to $2,272, zinc $29 to $2,290, and aluminum $95 to $2,420. Gold futures on Comex in NY jumped Friday after bleak U.S. employment figures strenthened expectations of further U.S. monetary easing, sending investors into the metal to hedge a weakening USD. "The gold market is interpreting it as keeping the spigot on for liquidity," says Jim Steel, senior vice president and metals analyst with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in NY. Spot gold $1,344.90/oz, down $1.95 early today from late Friday, when it bounced to an intraday high of $1,349.75 from a low of $1,324.95. Some other nations also undertaking monetary easing measures in a bid to support their exporters has also led to buying gold as a broader alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Everyone's trying to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; no-one's sure who's going to win," says Craig Ross, vice president of Chicago-based brokerage ApexFutures.com. "So do you want to be in paper, or in a metal that at least isn't going to hurt you no matter who wins?" Of other precious metals, silver slumped 72 cents Friday to $23.22/oz, though platinum up $6 at $1,701 and palladium $1 at $584. Nymex November crude futures down 99 cents on Friday at $82.84. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2126 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government bonds will be supported in Asia session says Tony Morriss, debt strategist at \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . Says weekend's meeting of IMF/G7 failed to add anything new for markets, so focus remains on U.S. looming QE measures. Adds heavy bond supply from U.S. this week could mute gains somewhat. December 3-year bonds now 95.08, unchanged. Range for session likely 95.04-95.15, Morriss says. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2109 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD supported in early trading but unlikely to extend too much higher for now. Grant Turley, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ, says IMF/G7 meetings over the weekend failed to resolve any of the lingering uncertainties in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so it's "business as usual", with USD weakness likely to persist amid ongoing chatter of forthcoming QE measures from the Fed. AUD/USD could extend to post-float highs of 0.9918, but any thrust toward parity likely to be left for European and U.S. session. Holiday in Japan likey to quieten Asia session. AUD/USD now 0.9893. Support at 0.9850, where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exporter demand is likely. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 10-10-10 1839ET ]\par \par \uc2 2055 GMT [Dow Jones]NZX-50 expected to open slightly higher as strong risk appetite continues in markets, says Macquarie Equities broker Brad Gordon; "It's reporting season in the U.S. this week and a number of majors are reporting --\uc2 General Electric\uc2 , \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 -- and that's going to be the driver of sentiment for this week as there is not a lot of local news." Sentiment will move bluechips Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), Telecom (TEL.NZ), \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ). Adds strong NZD/USD likely to continue as "data out of the U.S. was pretty ordinary and it all means there will probably be some money printing happening;" will weigh on exporter Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ). NZX-50 closed flat at 3232.66 points Friday. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2039 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD dropped to 15-year low against JPY Friday with data showing speculative investors ramped up their pro-yen bets by 72%, as speculation swirled Fed may soon act to stimulate a sagging economy, weighed down further by Friday's weak jobs data; USD/JPY fell as far as 81.72, putting investors on alert for signs of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength; traders said there were no signs of Japan in the market. Japanese Vice Finance Minister Rintaro Tamaki declined to comment. EUR/USD at 1.3929 late Friday vs 1.3912 late Thursday, USD/JPY at 82.08 vs 82.40, EUR/JPY at 114.33 vs 114.65, GBP/USD at 1.5953 vs 1.5862, USD/CHF at 0.9637 vs 0.9669. USD Index 77.255 vs 77.464. Treasurys maturing in next three to seven years were bit stronger after bleak U.S. jobs report added to already lofty expectations Fed will have to lend more support to economy this year; 3-year yields down 0.6 bp to 0.521%, 7-year yields down 0.7 bp to 1.739%. Stocks higher as bleak jobs report boosted expectations Fed will move to stimulate economic growth; \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  +5.7% after kicking off earnings season late Thursday with results that exceeded analysts' expectations; Dow +0.5%, Nasdaq +0.8%, Philly semicons +1.0%. November Nymex crude setttled up 99 cents to $82.66/bbl, lifted by weakness in dollar, concerns continuing strike at a French oil port may begin crimping supplies. December Comex gold rose $10.30 to $1,345.30/oz. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 10-10-10 1839ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101010e6aa0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
