\par\par \b \uc2 Australia, NZ dlrs jump on yen; Aussie up on rate view\b0\par\par\uc2 927 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 23:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie, kiwi jumps vs yen on talk of Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Aussie up 1.3 pct vs US$ for week, 6.8 percent for month\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi lags behind as poor data weighs\par \par \uc2 By Adrian Bathgate and Koh Gui Qing\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars jumped against the yen on Friday amid speculation Tokyo had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, while expectations of rate hikes in \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the Aussie buoyant on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  swirled in the market after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bolted nearly a yen against the U.S. dollar in just one minute in the early afternoon. [USD/]\par \par \uc2 Although Tokyo did not confirm it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the size and speed of the move convinced many it had. Traders are wary of any large yen sell order after Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week by selling the yen in waves to knock it off a 15-year peak.\par \par \uc2 Fears that Tokyo would launch another bout of yen-selling helped the Australian dollar to jump as far as 81.10 <AUDJPY=R>, from Thursday's 80.29. By late trade, it had retreated to 80.67.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar also climbed as far as 62.12 <NZDJPY=R>, from 61.62 earlier in the day, before easing to 61.74 in late session.\par \par \uc2 "I suspect Tokyo did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but I'm not sure if it would be all that successful," said Jonathan Cavenagh, an analyst at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cavenagh argued with many investors averse to buying the U.S. dollar right now for fear super-loose U.S. monetary policy would ease even further, Tokyo could struggle to pull the yen down against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I don't think Tokyo will be all that successful in getting a dramatic move higher," he said.\par \par \uc2 Given that, Cavenagh said he still expected the Australian dollar to rise to 84 yen in the next two months, helped in part by the Australian dollar's strength against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indeed, a robust domestic economy and expectations Australian interest rates could rise as soon as next month helped the Aussie dollar to hold ground at $0.9507, in sight of a two-year high.\par \par \uc2 It was up 1.3 percent for the week and 6.8 percent for the month.\par \par \uc2 So buoyant is the Australian dollar's outlook for the year, especially with the gloom hanging over the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that some analysts have rushed to dramatically revise their forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , for one, changed its December forecast for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to $0.97, from $0.88 previously.\par \par \uc2 It predicted the Aussie dollar could hit parity, or one-to-one against the U.S. dollar, by March. Just a month ago, many investors thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked pricey at $0.90 given fears of a second U.S. recession. The rates market was equally caught out by the turnaround in the mood in Australia in the past month. Investors have swung from betting on a rate cut in Australia this year to pricing in a 50-50 chance of a rate rise in October.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Deustche Bank went as far as to say the market could soon start to speculate if the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) would lift rates twice for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 "Our assessment remains that one hike this year is most likely," it said in a note to clients. "The hawkishness out of the RBA over the past week has seen us pull our November rate hike call forward to October."\par \par \uc2 Speculation of rate rises led the yield curve to flatten further. Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> gained 0.04 points to 95.120, and 10-year futures <YTCc1> up 0.055 points at 94.92.\par \par \uc2 The cash yield curve <0#AUBMK=> levelled to 16 basis points, its flattest in two years. Many analysts reckoned it would invert soon enough.\par \par \uc2 NO HIKES HERE\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the New Zealand dollar <NZD=D4> has fared less well. Dismal growth data this week has all but quashed any chance of a rise in rates from their current 3.0 percent this year. That held the New Zealand dollar down at $0.7290, a good way from an eight-month high of $0.7417 hit on Wednesday, but still up 0.3 percent for the week.\par \par \uc2 "The kiwi's had a tough week, with the GDP and terms of trade data unhelpful, plus the [RBNZ] hosing down any expectations of interest rate increases anytime soon means it was always going to be on the back foot," said Derek Rankin of Rankin Treasury.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi's struggles contrast with its Aussie neighbour and a hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Rankin said, with the Aussie gaining about six cents since the start of September, while the kiwi has only managed about half those gains.\par \par \uc2 That has seen the cross <AUDNZD=R> hit a five month high of NZ$1.3080 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Despite the weak outlook for the New Zealand economy the prospect of the Federal Reserve printing more money is set to keep the greenback low, Rankin said, meaning the kiwi was likely to remained hemmed in a $0.71 to $0.74 range over the short term.\par \par \uc2 NZ debt <0#NZTSY=> firmed, tracking U.S. Treasuries higher as weak jobs data stoked fears of further quantitative easing. [US/]\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-NEWZEALAND-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /BONDS(UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ND|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100926e69n000ew\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
