\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex May Fall To 18,250\b0\par\par\uc2 1,557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                10040.45  -133.96  -1.32% \par Nasdaq              2123.76   -35.87   -1.66% \par S&P 500             1051.87   -15.49   -1.45% \par FTSE 100            5155.95   -78.89   -1.51% \par Nikkei Stock        8905.16   -89.98   -1.00% \par Hang Seng           20659.48  +0.77     0.00% \par Kospi               1741.56   -18.97   -1.08% \par SGX Nifty           5499.50   -9.00    -0.16% \par August contract \par  \par USD/JPY             84.07-09     -1.30% \par Range               85.20-83.61 \par EUR/USD             1.2665-67    +0.08% \par Range               1.2718-1.2587 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Sept.    $6.746 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $1,233.40    +$4.90 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,211.70-$1,237.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $71.63       -$1.47 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bombay Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 30-share Sensitive Index could fall to 18,250, according to charts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's price action, after a couple of days of indecision, suggests that the bearish forces have taken control of the proceedings. A fall below minor support at 18,250 could lead to a test of major support at 18,050.\par \par \uc2 While there is a case for a minor downward correction, the short-term outlook still remains bullish. But a close below the July 30 intra-day low of 17,838 would indicate a reversal of the uptrend.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could test support at 5,480.\par \par \uc2 But the short-term trend would remain bullish as long as the Nifty stays above major support at 5,350, and a move past minor resistance at 5,525 will impart momentum to the uptrend.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks fell Tuesday, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Caterpillar leading a broad slide as a record drop in U.S. existing-home sales added to investors' worries about the health of the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the Dow's worst performer with a decline of 3.7%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 3% and Caterpillar slipped 2.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The declines followed the report of a 27.2% plunge in U.S. home resales in July to their lowest level in 15 years. The housing data represented the latest in a string of disappointing economic reports that have pointed to a weakening economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weak economic numbers have come despite a recent uptick in merger-and-acquisition activity, which would typically be seen as a sign of improving confidence in the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid the conflicting indicators, "people would rather be hoarding cash," said Seth Setrakian, partner and co-head of equity trading for \f28 \uc2 \f2 First New York Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a proprietary trading firm. "It's the uncertainty of whether a double-dip is coming that's causing people to be like this. We're consistently being bombarded with negative economic data, which is highlighted by the housing data we saw today. Yet on the other hand, you're seeing these significant premiums paid in M&A deals."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were lower Wednesday after Wall Street dropped Tuesday on more soft U.S. economic data, with Tokyo stocks remaining under pressure from a still-strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average, Australia's S&P/ASX 200, South Korea's Kospi Composite and New Zealand's NZX-50 were all down. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 28 points in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Tokyo market was hit by the generally depressed global sentiment and the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks were down across the board, with exporters denting the main index. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 1.4%, Canon lost 1.8% and Elpida Memory gave up 2.0%. Automakers weren't spared either, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 1.2%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  1.3% lower and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 1.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said investors continue to closely watch for any action from Japanese officials to curb the yen's rise, although recent comments from authorities indicated they were some steps away from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese daily the Nikkei reported Wednesday that the Bank of Japan is considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy to temper the soaring yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean stocks were pressured lower by weakness in some tech plays and Wall Street's drop. \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  was off 1.6%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 1.0%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hynix Semiconductor\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was buying $1.2634 against the U.S. dollar, from $1.2674 late Tuesday in New York, and at Y106.30 against the yen, from Y106.64.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen remained in focus after its surge against the greenback and euro Tuesday. The dollar slumped to a fresh 15-year low of Y83.58 immediately after the weak U.S. housing data, and was recently buying Y84.13, compared with Y84.15 in late New York trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The poor housing data caused investors to bid up gold prices as they fled to the perceived safety of the metal, and triggered a selloff in base metals on the London Metal Exchange.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,229.40 per troy ounce, down $1.00 from late New York trade Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October Nymex crude oil futures were down 18 cents at $71.45 per barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Aug. 25 \par 0900 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Prakash Steelage Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (533239.BY) listing \par 0915 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par                       of India chairman, Bank of India chairman, \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rural Electrification Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . chairman at \par                       Confederation of Indian Industry seminar \par 1030 IST New Delhi:   Mines minister at press meet \par 1130 IST New Delhi:   Apollo Group chairman at event \par 1130 IST Bangalore:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yakult Danone India\f28 \uc2 \f2  managing director at \par                       product launch \par 1130 IST New Delhi:   Suzuki Motorcycle India press meet \par 1100 IST New Delhi:   Platts Oil Forum Asia editorial director \par                       at press meet \par 1600 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mahindra & Mahindra\f28 \uc2 \f2  president for \par                       automotive and farm sector at press meet \par EARNINGS:             \f28 \uc2 \f2 MphasiS\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par 1100 GMT US:          Aug. 20 MBA weekly mortgage applications \par                       survey \par 1230 GMT US:          July durable goods \par 1400 GMT US:          July new residential sales \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par =UPDATE: JAPAN'S EXPORTS RISE IN JULY, BUT GROWTH PACE SLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's exports rose in July as shipments to Asia remained firm, but the pace of growth slowed for the fifth consecutive month, fanning fears that the country's broader economy may be losing steam. (published 0137 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: MUST TAKE PROPER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister said Wednesday the government must take proper steps against the rising yen if necessary, although he left it unclear whether he is considering conducting market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0028 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 AUSTRALIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 'S INDEPENDENT WILKIE: NO PREFERENCE FOR MAJOR PARTIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Andrew Wilkie, one of a number of non-party lawmakers who may play kingmaker in forming a minority government after Australia's weekend elections failed to deliver a victory to either main party, said Wednesday he doesn't favor either party. (published 2226 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN 2Q BUILDING ACTIVITY RISES 5.3% VS 1Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of building work completed in Australia rose 5.3% to A$21.42 billion in the second quarter of 2010 from the first quarter, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Wednesday. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =IFS: UK BUDGET TIGHTENING CLEARLY REGRESSIVE, HITS POOR HARDEST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The budget announced by the U.K. government in June failed to live up to its claim to be "progressive," and instead much of the burden of cutting the fiscal deficit will fall on poorer households, an influential think tank said Wednesday. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: IRELAND'S NTMA: REASONS FOR S&P'S DOWNGRADE 'FLAWED' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland's National Treasury Management Agency late Tuesday said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  reasons for downgrading Ireland to AA- from AA were "flawed," adding that they were based on "an extreme estimate" of EUR50 billion in bank recapitalization costs. (published 2231 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: ALIBABA.COM ANNOUNCES PURCHASE OF AUCTIVA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alibaba.com Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (1688.HK) said Tuesday it had acquired e-commerce software vendor Auctiva Corp., accelerating the Chinese company's push in the U.S. market and raising the specter of increased competition for \f28 \uc2 \f2 eBay Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (EBAY) (published 2234 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par POSCO: TO SIGN FINAL CONTRACT MONDAY TO BUY DAEWOO INTERNATIONAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean steelmaker Posco said Wednesday that it will sign a final contract to buy a controlling stake in trading and resources company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo International Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . held by state-run \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea Asset Management Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and other creditors. (published 0108 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MACARTHUR COAL: RAISES A$438.7M IN PLACEMENT AT A$11.50/SHARE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Macarthur Coal Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . successfully raised A$438.7 million through an institutional placement to buy the state-owned MDL 162 coking coal project, the company said Wednesday. (published 2307 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HOPSON DEVELOPMENT TO BUY STAKE IN GUANGZHOU SITE FOR CNY3.72 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hopson Development Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Tuesday it is planning to buy a stake in a site in Guangzhou for CNY3.72 billion, which will allow it to take part in the development of a commercial and residential complex. (published 0059 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Indian Media \par India's ONGC Divided On Cairn Counter-bid Idea - Business Standard \par India's Maruti Suzuki Looking At Making Cars For Other Manufacturers - Business Standard \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mahindra & Mahindra\f28 \uc2 \f2  Eyes Stake In Vayugrid's Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - DNA Money \par GTL, Reliance Communications To Convert INR60 Bln Loans Into Equity In Tower Entity - Economi