\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2010\par 09:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN DOLLAR, BONDS SLIDE ON HUNG PARLIAMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar traded lower in Asia Monday after the country's general election on the weekend left it unclear who will govern, though the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gained as the session wore on. (published 0710 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL DATA: CHINA CONFIRMS JULY CRUDE OIL IMPORTS 19 MLN TONS, DOWN 3.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's General Administration of Customs Monday confirmed that July crude oil imports fell 3.2% on year to 19 million metric tons, equivalent to 4.5 million barrels a day. (published 0730 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOV, PM KAN DISCUSSED RECENT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES BY PHONE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa and Prime Minister Naoto Kan conversed by telephone Monday morning to discuss the possible impact of a surging yen on Japan's economy, but the two didn't reportedly address the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0410 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 AUSTRALIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLL RESULT STILL UNCLEAR; FOCUS ON HASLUCK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The upper hand in negotiations with independent lawmakers to garner support for a minority government in Australia could come down to which main political party wins one closely-fought seat, analysts said Monday. (published 0714 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S PM: NEGOTIATIONS WITH INDEPENDENTS MAY TAKE TIME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talks with key independents over forming a minority government will take time, Australia's Prime Minister Julia Gillard said Monday. (published 0534 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA INDEPENDENTS PLAN MEETING - WINDSOR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The three independent lawmakers who will be critical to supporting a new minority government in Australia will meet either Monday or Tuesday to begin discussions on which party to support, one of the group, Tony Windsor, said Monday. (published 2350 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S GREENS BANDT TO MEET INDEPENDENTS MONDAY OR TUESDAY - SPOKESMAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adam Bandt, the newly elected Greens member to the lower House of Representatives and a likely key power broker in supporting a new government, is planning to meet with non-party lawmakers later Monday or Tuesday, a spokesman said Monday. (published 0221 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA AIMS TO REAP KRW1.9 TLN OVER 2011-2015 VIA TAX CHANGES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government Monday unveiled revisions to its tax system that aims to boost its tax coffers by KRW1.9 trillion over the next five years. (published 0730 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: SINGAPORE JULY CPI UP 3.1% VS +3.0% CONSENSUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Singapore rose a tad faster than expected in July due to higher transport, housing and food costs. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA OFFICIAL: NO PLAN YET TO CUT FISCAL YEAR BORROWING AIM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India has no plan to cut its borrowing target for the fiscal year ending March 31, 2011, a senior finance ministry official said Monday. (published 0435 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI 2Q GDP BEATS EXPECTATIONS TO GROW 0.2% ON QUARTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's economy continued to expand in the second quarter, beating market expectations, as surging exports helped counter the impact from the deadliest protests in the country's modern history. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINE TREASURY BILL YIELDS MIXED AT AUCTION; 91-DAY EASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on Philippine treasury bills were mixed at Monday's auction, with those on longer-dated paper rising in an apparent reflection of expectations that the earliest the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will hike policy rates is later this year. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/CHF CLOSE TO 1.3073 RECORD LOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Bears are in control of the short-term following Friday's push into fresh five-week lows below 1.2734, and support at 1.2664 is likely to face renewed pressure. The setback off the Aug. 6 peak at 1.3334 has been upgraded, and a main threat is for further weakness towards the 1.2605 area. Key support lies in the 1.2500 area should 1.2605 fail to hold. Resistance lies at 1.2780, but only regaining ground above 1.2833 would question the bearish outlook. (published 0750 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO CORRECT LOWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Consolidation off Friday's all-time high at 133.02 is underway, with 132.50 the immediate target. However, corrective weakness is limited to former range highs at 132.30, and dominant bulls will look to prompt a fresh attack on 133.02 from this 132.30 area. Once a near-term base has been formed, new highs above 133.02 cannot be ruled out, opening 133.36. (published 0656 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: AUSTRALIA POLL UNCERTAINTY MAY DENT CONSUMERS, SIDELINE RBA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A period of political uncertainty in Australia could bring economic and policy implications as already-cautious consumers cool their heels for a result, strengthening the case for interest rates to be left on hold. (published 0322 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LIKELY TO KEEP RATES STEADY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to leave policy settings as they are at its rate-setting meeting Thursday, with a benign inflation outlook providing the monetary authorities scope to stay accommodative amid a still patchy global economic recovery. (published 0330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN UP AS JAPAN PM, BOJ GOV HOLD ONLY PHONE TALK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen rose against the dollar in Asia Monday as Japan's prime minister and top central banker limited their dialogue on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  matters to a telephone conversation. This lead investors to think that Tokyo may not take immediate action to tame the yen's recent strength. (published 0542 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS UP; SEPTEMBER FOMC MEETING AWAITED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options rose in Asia Monday as demand for one-month protection against sharp fluctuation in the spot increased ahead of the U.S.'s Federal Open Market Committee meeting next month. (published 0539 GMT) [ 23-08-10 0704GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100823e68n000i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
