\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Aussie Funding Mkt Has Little To Fear -MONEY TALKS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2010\par 08:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0611 GMT [Dow Jones] Cost of funding in Australia's interbank market won't be following path of USD Libor given \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , resilient underlying economy. Biggest reason \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's short term funding market relaxed is RBA; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's proactive, no-nonsense approach has stood Australia's banking network well. Also, strong economic fundamentals, sense country remains protected against events in Europe also helping; Australia has much going for it right now, ranging from strong demand for its services, mining exports to flexible and responsive internal policy framework. Yield spread between Australian 3-month bank bill swap rate, overnight index swap has traded reasonably narrow range, last around 39 bps. Spread traded peak of 110 bps in October 2008, unlikely to revisit those levels in hurry. See =MONEY TALKS: Australia's Funding Market Has Few Reasons For Fear (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD, EUR/JPY off lows as Asian short-term players cover EUR-shorts, taking cues from solid Asian shares, says dealer at major Tokyo bank. Nikkei last +1.3%. "The euro's recent gains are just driven by short-covering of euro-positions, but no players want to purchase the euro" for longer-term bets amid gloomy outlook for euro zone's debt issues. Dealer says EUR's downtrend may persist as deep-rooted problems in fiscally-troubled euro-zone nations may not be solved easily. Says speculators eager to sell EUR if negative news emerges. Says EUR/USD's upside will likely be limited around 1.2450 in global day, could fall toward 1.2250 vs last 1.2330. EUR/JPY's ceiling at 113.00; could fall toward 111.50 vs 112.37. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF will remain under pressure as Swiss data is likely to confirm the robust recovery in the economy, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . The Swiss trade data for April is due Friday as well as the KOF leading indicator, which should confirm Swiss growth is outpacing that in the euro region, says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Lutz Karpowitz. "While the market is quieter early Friday, the upward pressure on the franc is likely to remain," he says. EUR/CHF is at 1.4245. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher with resources, energy stocks leading gains in host of markets; Nikkei +1.3%, S&P/ASX +1.6%, Kospi 0.8% higher, Shanghai Composite down 0.4%, NZX-50 +0.4%, HSI +1.8%, Taiex +0.7%, Sensex +0.8%. Markets in Singapore, Indonesia, Thailand, Malaysia closed for public holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR weaker vs USD on profit-taking after sharp gains overnight; EUR/USD at 1.2325 vs 1.2356 late in New York Thursday, EUR/JPY at 112.43 vs 112.30. USD/JPY at 91.25 vs 90.90. Slew of data from Japan: core consumer price index down 1.5% on-year in April vs 1.2% slide in March, 1.4% fall expected. Jobless rate higher at 5.1% in April vs 5.0% previous month, 5.0% tipped. April retail sales +4.9% on-year, while all household spending falls 0.7% on-year vs +2.3% forecast after 4.4% rise in March. Bank of Korea Manufacturers' Outlook Index down at 104 for June vs 7-year high of 107 for May. NZ residential building permits issued in April up seasonally adjusted 8.5% on-month vs revised +0.1% in March. Spot gold at $1,213.30/oz, up $1.30 vs NY close. July Nymex crude oil futures up 14 cents at $74.69/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones]--EUR/CHF has stabilized, but the cross remains shaky, \uc2 Landesbank Baden-Wuerttemberg\uc2  says. The downside potential is seen at 1.4005. Meanwhile, a break of 1.4350 may send the pair towards 1.4410. EUR/CHF stands at 1.4247. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end tad lower but off lows as stocks pare some gains, and likely on buying at lows by banks, says local brokerage trader. "Yields have rebounded recently so some were buying at (yield) highs," he says; adds, buying by banks may signal players turn positive on bond market again as euro-zone sovereign debt issues haven't been resolved yet. Market also to watch appetite for corporate bonds of Taiwan Power, island's largest issuer with auction results due shortly. Yield on reopened 10-year bond ends up at 1.3767% vs 1.3699% in when-issued trade, tipped in 1.35%-1.38% band Monday; 5-year ends up at 0.9855% vs 0.9807%. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +12.6% at HK$2.882 billion at interval, 7.4% of market total, vs 6.9% Thursday's half-day, suggests further upside in HSI likely limited after 1.8% rise midday. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) leads with HK$194.41 million, followed by Tracker Fund (2800.HK) with HK$172.50 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$157.54 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$125.14 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$88.92 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.7% at 7295.32, boosted by gains on regional bourses, but off early high of 7364.01 as tepid trading volume indicates many players still sidelined, says Capital Securities assistant VP Diana Wu. "Worries over Korea are easing but the market is still watching the situation with euro-zone sovereign debt," she says, tips Taiex to trade between 5-day MA of 7211 and 10-day MA of 7350 Monday with tech shares likely in focus as major trade show Computex opens next week. Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) ends down 1.2% to NT$127.0 after spate of suicides at its Shenzhen plant but Acer (2353.TW) +6.9% to NT$80.50 after unveiling yesterday its first e-reader, touchscreen tablet computer; AU Opto (2409.TW) +2.0% at NT$30.95 after firm announces it will build US$700 million solar-cell plant. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan April industrial output likely +2.7% on month, according to median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones; would mark 2nd straight month of gains, mostly due to continuing robust demand from overseas. In March, output revised to +1.2%. Besides headline figure, Dai-ichi Life Research Institute's senior economist Yoshiki Shinke says he will pay attention to May, June forecast figures in order to gauge pace of output growth ahead. Data due 2350 GMT Sunday. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] 1-month ATM EUR/JPY implied volatilities down at 20.05%/20.85% from 21.10%/21.90% as lull in recent falls in spot dents demand for hedges against further slides. One player sells 3-month ATM EUR/JPY straddle option contract with implied volatility of 19.50%, options dealer at major Japanese bank says. EUR/JPY last 112.05. 1-month ATM USD/JPY implied volatilities also down, at 12.75%/13.45% from 13.30%/14.00%, after spot earlier marks 1-week high at 91.36; pair last 91.10. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0515 GMT [Dow Jones] Telecom NZ (TEL.NZ) has little choice but to split its retail, network business, but separation will cost the company it's size and earning potential, says DJ's Focus. Structural separation, analysts estimate will cost TEL around NZ$225 million, would be more than offset by benefits from being part of NZ$3 billion high speed fiber network; as it stands TEL can't participate in build due to its retail arm. Telecom Chief Executive Reynolds says separation economically challenging but TEL wanted to be part of fiber future. Analyst say separation may see TEL falling out of NZX-10, share price taking a hit. See: "=FOCUS: Telecom NZ Should Split To Remain Viable Player Longer Term." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] In a sign of just how heated the debate between Australia's Rudd Labor government and the country's powerful mining lobby is becoming over a controversial proposal to impose a 40% tax on mining profits, Canberra invokes "emergency" powers to embark on its own advertising campaign to counter the full page ads mining lobbyists have been running, panning the tax proposal. Normally, political parties are constrained from running campaigns for party political purposes, except in national emergency or extreme urgency. "As the (tax) changes also affect the value of capital assets and impact on financial markets, I'm satisfied that a compelling reason for an exemption exists, particularly given the nature and extent of misinformation against a backdrop of continuing market volatility," Cabinet Secretary Ludwig says in approving the campaign. Could be a serious misstep for PM Rudd, however, if voters (who'll foot the bill for the taxpayer-funded ad campaign) don't agree that his struggle to explain the mining tax policy quite constitutes a national crisis. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 28, 2010 02:11 ET (06:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100528e65s000rp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
