\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES \highlight\uc2 : Euro Rises, Adds To First Weekly Gain In Five\b0\par\par\uc2 By Deborah Levine  \par \uc2 868 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 21:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose Friday versus the dollar, pushing the shared \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its first weekly gain in five, as traders continued closing so-called short positions in the euro -- bets that it had further to fall.\par \par \uc2 The euro touched a four-year low, under the $1.22 mark, earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The euro improved after both houses of the Germany parliament approved the country's contribution to a regional bailout plan -- removing some political risk involving big contributors of funds needed by Greece to remain solvent.\par \par \uc2 The euro (CUR_EURUSD) rose to $1.2581, up from $1.2550 late Thursday. Earlier, it rose as high as $1.2672.\par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese yen, the euro (CUR_EURYEN) traded at \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A5113.05, from \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A5112.99 on Thursday, a day during which the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped as low as \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A5109.50 -- its weakest since 2001.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index (DXY), which tracks the U.S. unit against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell to 85.397, down from 85.637 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar (CUR_USDYEN) traded at \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A589.87, compared to \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A590.01 late Thursday, when it fell to a two-week low of \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A588.95.\par \par \uc2 The British pound (CUR_GBPUSD) bought $1.4472, from $1.4417 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Accompanying positions shorting the euro were long positions in risky trades that have also been unwound, said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 's Arrop Chatterjee. Seen in that light, "market moves this week can be seen as risk reducing," the strategist said.\par \par \uc2 As expected, both the upper and the lower house of Germany's parliament voted to approve its contribution to the 750 billion bailout plan put together by the European Union and the International Monetary Fund for euro-zone nations facing debt problems.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries\par \par \uc2 Analysts said fear that central banks would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as extremely oversold technical conditions likely were main factors in the euro's recent bounce.\par \par \uc2 "Those seeing being short euro as a free pass to make money have been and will continue to be forced to cover in the days and perhaps weeks ahead," said John Spinello, Treasury strategist at Jefferies & Co.\par \par \uc2 The euro held most of the day's gains after Herman Van Rompuy, president of the European Council said officials agreed they need stronger economic governance and more effective crisis management.\par \par \uc2 "European leaders need to be sending a concerted, united message and at no time during this crisis has this been the case," said Michael Hewson, strategist at CMC Markets, before the meeting ended. "This is the conundrum that investors are currently wrestling with, despite the rally in the euro over the last couple of days."\par \par \uc2 Speculation that "covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already contributed to the rebound isn't supported by "any evidence so far," said Beat Siegenthaler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks would be likely to slow the euro's overall slide at best, strategists contend.\par \par \uc2 Siegenthaler noted that the \uc2 UBS\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flow monitor shows that overall shorts in euro crosses reached an all-time high of $63 billion in the week to May 7, with 85% of them in the euro/U.S. dollar cross.\par \par \uc2 "In such a situation, it did not take much to set in motion a chain reaction of forced euro buying as stop losses were triggered en masse," Siegenthaler said.\par \par \uc2 'Anything can happen'\par \par \uc2 Also, traders are "heading to a weekend when anything can happen," said David Watt, senior fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . "Better to sit tight with the profits that have been booked and prepare for tactical moves next week."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Australian dollar (CUR_AUDUSD) is headed for its worst week since October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Australia's formerly high-flying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken a heavy hit as worries about Europe's sovereign-debt crisis stirred turmoil in global markets.\par \par \uc2 Traders are also changing between expressing risk aversion by selling commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including those of Australia, Canada and New Zealand -- instead of by selling the euro and British pound, said Ashraf Laidi, chief market strategist at CMC Markets.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Australian dollar rose 1.9% to 82.67 U.S. cents, which strategists attributed to market talk about suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  inspired some short-covering.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries that export commodities were among the favored ones when the global economy was on the mend, amid expectations that's demand for commodities would increase. As problems in Europe have many rethinking that recovery, commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have declined.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has risen against the Canadian dollar (C_CAD) for the third week in four, after Thursday closing at the highest level against the loonie since February.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar is also near its highest level against the New Zealand dollar (C_NZD) since September.\par \par \uc2 For the day, the Australian unit was also up about 2.1% against the yen, changing hands at \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A574.19.\par \par \uc2 The aussie is still near its lowest level against the yen since July 2009, however.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 21, 2010 15:31 ET (19:31 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100521e65l0055l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
