\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro rises in choppy trade, Aussie surges\b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 02:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Short-covering helps buoy euro\par \par \uc2 * Aussie jumps on jitters over possible RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Japanese importer bids nudge dollar up vs yen\par \par \uc2 * Low market liquidity exacerbates moves\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 21 (Reuters) - The euro rose on Friday as a bout of short-covering extended after its slide to a four-year low earlier this week, while the Australian dollar climbed on talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar slid initially on loss-cut selling against the yen by Japanese retail margin traders, but later rebounded to jump 3 percent against the yen, with moves exacerbated by thin market conditions.\par \par \uc2 Market players said the Australian dollar was lifted by talk that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the Australian dollar. The RBA said that it did not comment on market moves.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar jumped 3.1 percent against the yen to 74.11 yen and surged 1.3 percent against the dollar to $0.8234, having pulled up from a 10-month low struck earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rebounded after taking a beating earlier this week, especially against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The euro has jumped about 8 percent against the Aussie in the past three days on a flurry of short-covering by hedge funds and other short-term traders as well as fund repatriation by European investors, traders said.\par \par \uc2 A deepening slide in global equities has helped to prompt the dash for the exits by jittery investors and given some respite to the euro, which has slid 12 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "The impact spilled over into various places and led to moves to cut losses in euro/Swiss franc, euro/Aussie or sterling/Aussie," said Minoru Shioiri, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading manager at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.6 percent to $1.2561, clawing back toward a peak near $1.2600 struck the previous day, and well above its four-year trough of $1.2143 marked on trading platform EBS earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The euro climbed 1 percent against the yen to 113.15 yen, rebounding from an 8-\u189\'00\'BD year low of 109.47 yen hit on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Against the Australian dollar, the euro stood at 1.5238.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.5 percent against the yen to 90.08 yen, getting some support from dollar buying by Japanese importers, market players said. (Additional reporting by Anirban Nag in Sydney; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100521e65l0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
