\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr jumps from 10mth lows but outlook marred\b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2010\par 00:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie rallies on buying from funds, Japanese, Asian c.banks\par \par \uc2 * But still in biggest weekly fall since Oct. 2008\par \par \uc2 * Seen weakening further to $0.77 in coming weeks\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi also rebounds but less dramatically\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, May 21 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar surged three percent on Friday, with hedge funds and Asian central banks cited as buyers after its fast slide this week rendered it attractive for those in for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 A bout of short-covering further boosted the rally, helping the Australian dollar to rebound from 10-month lows to as far as $0.8373. It retreated slightly by early evening to $0.8291, little changed from levels seen here late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The panic selling over the last two sessions is done for now," said Greg Gibbs, an analyst at RBS. "This is a good time to buy if you have a medium to long-term view."\par \par \uc2 Still, Reuters matching data showed trading volumes behind the Aussie's bounce on Friday were far thinner than volumes seen during the sell-off earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 That suggested the rally was exaggerated by an illiquid market, and only a minority thought the Aussie was a bargain now, especially since it was among the most bought major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  before this week's sell down.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the Aussie rallied to 74.69 <AUDJPY=>, up nearly 4 percent from a low of 73.06 seen earlier Friday. Traders said Japanese retail and margin investors led the buying.\par \par \uc2 Friday's outsized moves were a symptom of the violent swings in the Aussie this week, one of the most volatile weeks since the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Aussie dived 6.4 percent against the U.S. dollar, its worse performance in a week since October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Its drop against the yen was even more dramatic since it is one of the favoured carry-trade plays. The Aussie slumped 8.5 percent on the yen for the week, also the worse weekly since October 2008.\par \par \uc2 One-month implied volatility on Friday hovered near levels not seen in more than a year at 24.75 percent, slightly under Thursday's high of 25.7. <AUDVOL=ICAP>\par \par \uc2 The wild swings in prices stirred talk on Friday the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the bruised Aussie, which many reckoned could fall further. When asked, the RBA said it does not comment on market movements.\par \par \uc2 The RBA has said in the past it only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  when when the market is disorderly, which is characterised by high volatility, sharply widening bid-ask spreads, or erratic movements. It has said it does not target specific Aussie levels.\par \par \uc2 Over the coming months however, some analysts reckoned the Aussie is likely to fall further as investors continue to pare their bull bets.\par \par \uc2 Losses were seen deepening to $0.7707, the 50 percent retracement of its rise from October 2008 lows to November 2009 highs.\par \par \uc2 KIWI HAD MILDER SELL-OFF\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar <NZD=D4> also rebounded strongly gaining 1.5 percent against the U.S. dollar, trading between $0.6641 and $0.6785 through the session. It had hit a nine-month low of $0.6619 overnight as moved in tandem with the neighbouring Aussie dollar.\par \par \uc2 For the week the kiwi lost around 4.5 percent against the U.S. dollar, but an analyst said it was too soon to say it was headed for a sustained fall.\par \par \uc2 "In the absence of further meltdown in global equities, we suspect NZ dollar dips towards $0.6500-0.6600 will be short-lived," said Bank of NZ strategist Mike Jones.\par \par \uc2 Going into the weekend the kiwi is seen trading in a $0.6600 to $0.6800 range.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi, which had not suffered as badly as the neighbouring Aussie and thus risen to a seven-month high on the cross rate <NZDAUD=R>, did not gain as strongly as the Aussie in the rebound and was down around 1.5 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 Against the euro, kiwi moved off a one month low to be little changed from the previous session at 0.5370 <NZDEUR=R>, while it leaped more than 2 percent against the safe haven yen <NZDJPY=R> from a 10-month low.\par \par \uc2 The volatility in the markets was manifest in debt <NZDIRS> as well with the two-year swap yield at one stage falling 21 basis points before closing down 1 basis point. The 10-year yield also came back to close three basis points lower from being 10 basis points lower.\par \par \uc2 Both trimmed those falls to close one basis point and three basis points respectively.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the pricing of the first NZ rate rise on June 10 has fallen to around 52 percent from 70 percent <CSRBNZ=CSAU> at the start of the day.\par \par \uc2 "There is a general flight to safety...therefore the Reserve bank will not hike in June, that's the view," said \uc2 Westpac\uc2  strategist Imre Speizer.\par \par \uc2 However, a new Reuters poll showed virtually no change in expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start raising rates next week. See [NZ/POLL]\par \par \uc2 Data showing migration gains in April down to a 16-month low in April, while consumer confidence improved to its best level in three months had no impact. See [ID:nWEL004052] and [ID:nnSGE64J01J] (Reporting by Koh Gui Qing in SYDNEY and Gyles Beckford in WELLINGTON; Editing by Ed Davies)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-NEWZEALAND-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /BONDS (UPDATE2)|LANGEN|Table|ND