\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Investor Worries Push Australian Dollar To 8-Month Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos and James Glynn   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2010\par 14:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions that bet on global growth came undone Thursday, sinking the Australian dollar to an eight-month low, and reflecting concerns that global growth is more fragile than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 At its lowest Thursday, the Australian dollar was down more than 2.5% against the U.S. dollar and is now down a swift 11% since May 3. During its skid in Asian hours on Thursday, it touched its lowest level since September 2009 at 82.56 U.S. cents, down from a recent high in mid-April of 93.82 U.S. cents. By late in the Asian trading day it was at 83.55 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar is considered a barometer of global risk appetite. Its fall reflects the quick change in mood, as Europe's debt problems and China's monetary tightening plans cloud expectations for the global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The pace of the move is "certainly surprising and quite scary," said Ray Farris, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in London. He said uncertainty about regulations affecting financial markets, triggered by the German ban on naked short selling and an expected vote in the U.S. Senate on regulatory reform, has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on the back of their heels. "It's clear that markets have been caught significantly off guard," he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also reacting to indications from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 , the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that Australia will take a break from a steady series of interest-rate increases. Higher interest rates are a key attraction for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players. The recent drop in commodity prices on fears of a Chinese growth slowdown also hurts mineral-rich Australia.\par \par \uc2 The rapid unwinding raised the prospect that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by buying Australian dollars in order to slow the speed of the drop, according to Sue Trinh, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Rates of change in the exchange rate matter more for central banks like RBA as opposed to the outright level," she said. If the Australian dollar "keeps tanking at the current rate, it is possible the RBA would look to inject liquidity in the market," she said.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't comment when asked if the RBA had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The RBA's last confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in late 2008, when it was successful in stopping the Australian dollar from falling below 60 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 The Korean won, also seen as a beneficiary of global growth, flailed Thursday on fears of Europe's debt problems and on bellicose talk from North Korea over the March sinking of a South Korean submarine. Korean banks and business rely on European banks more than others in the region. The U.S. dollar was 2.5% stronger against the won Thursday. The Japanese yen gained 2.7% versus the won.\par \par \uc2 While much of the world's financial angst emanates from Europe's debt problems, the euro was an unlikely beneficiary of the reduction of risk Wednesday and Thursday. It was up nearly 2% against the Australian dollar Thursday and also higher against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to growth such as the Canadian dollar.\par \par \uc2 Driving the euro higher was an undoing of what are known as carry trades, in which investors borrow funds in euros or other low-interest-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and invest them in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with better growth prospects. According to data from the U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission, more investors were betting on the Australian dollar rising than any other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. Japanese retail investors also have record high levels of Australian dollar exposure, according to market data examined by \uc2 Barclays Capital\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist David Forrester.\par \par \uc2 "The euro rally isn't that people like the euro," said Farris, of \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "Investors have decided they want out of risk." The way to remove that risk from portfolios is to pay back the euro loans by selling the Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 A similar dynamic was in place with the Japanese yen. The Australian dollar fell as much as 3.5% against the yen before recovering somewhat. It traded at Y76.06 in European morning trading. During the worst of the financial panic in the second half of 2008, the unwinding of carry trades sent the Aussie dollar into a tailspin. It lost more than a third of its value against the U.S. dollar and more than 40% against the yen within the span of a few months. Some say the Australian dollar selling went too far.\par \par \uc2 "The environment is not yet ripe to get truly bearish on the Australian dollar," said Commonwealth Bank Strategist Richard Grace. There are positives on the horizon, namely a better outlook for the U.S. and a calming of the Greek crisis, he said. He's forecasting a return to $0.87.\par \par \uc2 -By Alex Frangos and James Glynn, The Wall Street Journal; Alex.Frangos@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (Enda Curran in Sydney contributed to this article.) [ 05-20-10 0834ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100520e65k00050\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
