\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2010\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S RBA RAISES INFLATION, GROWTH FORECASTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Friday revised up its forecasts for inflation and economic growth, signaling that more interest rate increases will be needed to temper a surge in activity fueled by a hefty commodity price upswing and limited spare capacity. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA THINK-TANK FORECASTS 2Q GDP GROWTH 10.7% - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy is estimated to grow around 10.7% in the second quarter, the state-run China Securities Journal said Friday, citing a government think tank. (published 2348 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA APRIL NEW LOANS AROUND CNY700 BLN - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banks in China extended around CNY700 billion worth of new loans in April, up from CNY510.7 billion in March, the state-run Shanghai Securities News reported Friday, citing several unnamed sources. (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA GOVERNMENT: EUROPE FISCAL WOES TO HAVE LIMITED DIRECT IMPACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government on Friday said that the fiscal woes of southern European states will have limited direct impact on the domestic financial market. (published 0252 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: BOJ INJECTS SAME-DAY FUNDS; FIRST TIME SINCE DECEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan injected Y2 trillion into the short-term money market Friday via a same-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help calm domestic financial markets after European sovereign debt fears pushed up the yen steeply overnight and weighed on Tokyo shares. (published 0152 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN HIRANO: WILL WATCH \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS TO SEE IF ACTION NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remarks from Chief Cabinet Secretary Hirofumi Hirano came after the dollar tumbled Thursday to Y87.95, the lowest since Y87.73 on Dec. 10, amid worries over Greece's debt turmoil and sharp drops in U.S. stock markets. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stood at Y91.73 around 1027 GMT. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN'S KAN: LITTLE CHANCE OF G-7 ACTION TO LIFT EURO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister on Friday said he and other Group of Seven finance ministers are planning a telephone conference later in the day, but he brushed aside speculation that the group may use it to mull joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the beleaguered euro. (published 0245 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 AUSTRALIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PM: MARKETS JUDGE GREEK PLAN TO BE INADEQUATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Prime Minister Kevin Rudd said Friday global financial markets have judged the Greek rescue plan as inadequate, and as a result financial market stability will be at the core of the agenda for the forthcoming Group of 20 leaders' meeting in Canada in June. (published 0044 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA PERFORMANCE OF CONSTRUCTION INDEX +4.8% TO 55.8 IN APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian construction sector activity was boosted in April by a rise in new orders and increased labor demand, according to a survey published Friday. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ GOVT 9-MONTH CORE OPERATING DEFICIT BEATS FORECASTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The New Zealand government's core operating deficit tracked better than forecast in the nine months ended March 31, but underlying tax revenue remained weak as the country gradually emerges from a prolonged downturn. (published 0228 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AQUINO WIDENS LEAD IN PHILIPPINE PRESIDENTIAL RACE, OPINION POLL SAYS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benigno Aquino is set for an historic win in next week's Philippine presidential election after widening an already big lead over his rivals, according to an opinion poll released Friday. (published 0119 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ: EURO ZONE LEADERS TO ADDRESS DEBT FEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone summit set for Friday is shaping up to be a major effort to demonstrate that European policy makers can get the continent's financial problems under control and sooth markets. (published 0105 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: GILTS, STERLING TEMPER GAINS AS HUNG PARLIAMENT LOOMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fading hopes of a overall majority for the opposition Conservative Party has resulted in waning enthusiasm in the U.K. markets Friday, with sterling and gilts selling off highs. Still, the sharp falls in U.S. equities late Thursday have tended to overshadow the ongoing U.K. general election. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EARLY UK ELECTION RESULTS POINT TO HUNG PARLIAMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. opposition Conservative Party looks set to win the most seats in Thursday's general election but fall short of a parliamentary majority. (published 0231 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PERU'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  INCREASES REFERENCE RATE TO 1.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Central Reserve Bank of Peru on Thursday made a surprise 25% basis point increase in its policy rate. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RENHE DELAYS PRICING 5-YEAR DOLLAR BOND DUE TO MARKET CONDITION -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Renhe Commercial Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a developer of underground shopping malls in China, has decided to delay pricing its five-year dollar-denominated global bond owing to unfavorable market conditions, a person familiar with the plan said Friday. (published 0212 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =PREVIEW: MALAYSIA MARCH INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT LIKELY +13.5% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's industrial output growth in March was likely substantially stronger than the month before on robust external demand, economists said. (published 0208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR, EURO RECOVER IN EARLY ASIA BUT ANALYSTS WARN OF MORE VOLATILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro and U.S. dollar recovered slightly in early Asian trading Friday but analysts said they expect further volatility once Asian equities markets open and reflect the U.S. stockmarket sell-off. (published 2255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(5/7) EDITORIAL: GREECE AND CONTAGION THIS SHOULDN'T BE A REPEAT OF 2008. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yesterday's death-defying plunge in stocks was due -- pick your favorite -- to fear of contagion from Greece, a mistaken sell order from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the financial regulation bill in Congress, or the rampage of machine trading. The regulators are sure to tell us about any erroneous trades, so our point today is to focus on the risk of contagion. (published 0012 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(5/7) HEARD ON THE STREET: SOVEREIGN WOES SOCK BULL MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In theory, riots in Athens shouldn't cause the Dow to plunge. But the European debt crisis is undermining two, near-canonical assumptions that underpin markets. (published 0033 GMT) [ 07-05-10 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100507e6570009s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
