\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Is  \highlight22\uc2 Australia \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Koh Gui Qing   \par \uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2009\par 07:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, July 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week it sold a record net A$1.9 billion ($1.6 billion) of Australian dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, feeding talk among investors that perhaps it thinks the Aussie has risen too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie has risen nearly 17 percent against the U.S. dollar so far this year as stabilising financial markets and hopes for a global economic recovery prompt investors to seek out higher-risk, higher-yielding assets again.\par \par \uc2 Here are some questions and answbers about the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's (RBA) transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and their implications for the Aussie dollar <AUD=D4>.\par \par \uc2 DOES JUNE'S RECORD SELLING OF AUSSIE COUNT AS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Yes and no, depending on who you talk to. To the RBA, its sales of the Aussie, in May and June, are efforts to "rebuild reserves" and do not count as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The RBA says it often acts as a money changer for Australia's government, supplying it with foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay for transactions. When that happens, the RBA sells the Aussie to replenish its reserves. This usually happens every month.\par \par \uc2 That is not to say the RBA is not opportunistic in these routine trades. For instance, it said it made use of the Aussie's strength in June to sell more of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it needed to.\par \par \uc2 To the market though, that is a form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and some investors think the RBA is signalling the Aussie is over-bought. Some traders suspect the RBA may still be selling the Aussie when it is around $0.8150.\par \par \uc2 "I consider it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kieran Davies, chief economist at RBS. "The amount they are selling is so large that they are clearly leaning into the Aussie's strength."\par \par \uc2 WHAT IS THE RBA'S TRACK RECORD AS A TRADER?\par \par \uc2 The RBA made a profit of A$138 million in the financial year ending June 2008 selling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its annual report showed. That was the eighth straight year that it had made money from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, though profits were also at their lowest level in eight years.\par \par \uc2 Still, the RBA's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades since the financial crisis suggests it will make a tidy profit this year.\par \par \uc2 It bought a record A$3.15 billion in October when the Aussie slumped to a 5-\u189\'00\'BD year low near 60 U.S. cents, and sold a record amount in June when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an eight-month high of $0.8263.\par \par \uc2 The RBA says it usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the peaks and troughs of the Aussie's trading cycles.\par \par \uc2 WHY DOES THE RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The RBA says it seeks to influence the value of the Aussie when it is not in line with fundamentals, or when markets are disorderly. \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are almost always focused on the Aussie's rate against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The bank says the Aussie is judged to have "overshot" its value when it has moved a "long way" such that the exchange rate is not reasonable given economic and financial conditions.\par \par \uc2 It says it trades the Aussie by buying low and selling high, believing that this helps to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over time.\par \par \uc2 The RBA also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the market when it has become disorderly -- characterised by high volatility, sharply widening bid-ask spreads, or erratic movements.\par \par \uc2 It announced its last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October at the height of the financial crisis, after \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapsed.\par \par \uc2 The RBA bought a record A$3.15 billion then to stabilise the Aussie, which plunged 40 percent as investors fled riskier, leveraged trades.\par \par \uc2 It said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide liquidity because traders were unwilling to buy the Aussie as it went into free fall.\par \par \uc2 The RBA, however, states very clearly that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  do not aim to defend a specific level of the exchange rate, unsurprising since the bank does not have the means to do so.\par \par \uc2 The RBA has around $48 billion in reserves, small beer compared the daily $175-billion turnover in Aussie trade, according to 2007 data from the Bank for International Settlements.\par \par \uc2 HOW DOES THE RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 That depends on how much the RBA wants investors to know it is in the market. The most low-key form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to get other banks to trade its orders, concealing the RBA's presence. It routinely buys foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the government and can tack on orders for its own account.\par \par \uc2 The most aggressive form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  entails the RBA phoning banks quoting bids or offers for the Aussie.\par \par \uc2 Banks that get a call from the RBA will be forced to shift their quotes to make them less attractive to avoid trading with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This pushes the Aussie in the direction that the RBA wants.\par \par \uc2 The RBA says over the years it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  less frequently but on a larger scale. The largest daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was around A$1.2 billion in 2004, up from A$250 million in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Still, the scale the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  belies its impact since talk of the RBA's presence in markets is enough to alter trading behaviours. The RBA calls this the "announcement effect". (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|AUF|ND|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PCM   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090723e57n000b4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
