\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Australia c.bank bought record A$ amid Oct plunge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wayne Cole   \par \uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2008\par 01:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, analyst reaction)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Nov 20 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy a record amount of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October as turmoil in global markets sent the Aussie tumbling to five-year lows, data out on Thursday showed.\par \par \uc2 Showing its commitment to keeping markets orderly in extraordinary times, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) bought a net A$3.15 billion ($2.0 billion) in October. That was the largest amount ever bought in a single month and the first purchase since 2001.\par \par \uc2 "It's a jaw-dropping number," said Sean Callow, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . "it's a lot more than we expected and shows just how illiquid and difficult markets were in October."\par \par \uc2 At the time of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it equalled almost 10 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's entire official reserves, which then only totalled A$36 billion. Ironically, the slide in the local dollar has since boosted the value of the RBA's reserves to A$44.9 billion even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Unlike many of its Asian neighbours, \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has never favoured holding large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves preferring to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely and find its own level.\par \par \uc2 However, in October conditions became extreme as turmoil in global markets and fears of a world recession sent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crashing by 25 percent over the month to hit five-year lows near 60 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 The RBA has since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically when the market has become disorderly. Last week it bought the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around $0.6350 <AUD=> after it dived around 6 U.S. cents in just a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced as far as $0.6697 after last week's action, but by Thursday it was back down at $0.6390 as world stocks markets dived and investors shunned all risk.\par \par \uc2 In the minutes of its November policy meeting released this week, the RBA said the drop in the dollar had been driven by gloom over the global economy, sharp falls in the prices of resources that Australia exports, and a general unwinding of leveraged positions.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was also used by sellers as a proxy for less liquid investments, including emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Volatility in the exchange rate reached unprecedented levels in October," the RBA said. It had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on several occasions to provide liquidity but this was "not designed to defend any particular level of the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 In large part this recognises the reality that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with reserves of A$44 billion simply cannot stand against a global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that trades over $3 trillion a day.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few years, the RBA has generally been a net seller of Australian dollars, rebuilding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to take advantage of a long rally that took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as high as $0.9849 in July this yea