\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr finds support, but mood still bearish\b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2008\par 22:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Nov 19 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar was holding relatively steady on Wednesday after a choppy offshore session saw it bounce as U.S. stocks rallied, and investors awaited speeches from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the day.\par \par \uc2 * The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hovering around $0.6510 <AUD=>, having recovered from a $0.6405 low as Wall Street <.SPX> made one of its mysterious late charges <.N>.\par \par \uc2 * Support seen around $0.6350 and the possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) is putting a floor under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the moment.\par \par \uc2 * Aussie also finding some support around 61.75 yen <AUDJPY=R>, allowing it to creep up to 63.25, though fresh sell orders are seen at 64.00.\par \par \uc2 * Overall, sentiment remains bearish on the Aussie given \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the prospect of global recession -- there were more dismal\par housing data out of the U.S. on Tuesday [nN18202597] -- and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 expectations of more aggressive rate cuts here.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  features large on Wednesday with RBA Governor Glenn Stevens speaking on "The Economic Situation" at 8:35 p.m. (0935 GMT).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * He is preceded by Assistant Governor Malcolm Edey speaking on "The Economy in Late 2008: Conditions and Prospects" at 2:50 p.m. (0350 GMT).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Analysts are hoping for guidance on how much they might cut interest rates at the December policy meeting, though events are moving so fast that the comments might have a short shelf-life.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * The market is already pricing in a cut of at least 75 basis points in the 5.25 percent cash rate, and further drastic easing to 3.5 percent next year, so it might be vulnerable to a pullback if the governors are less than outright bearish.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Australian bond futures traded firmer, buoyed by a strong performance from U.S Treasuries which saw two-year yields touch their lowest levels since 2003.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> rose 0.055 points to 96.225, while the 10-year contract added 0.045 to 95.075.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Commodities were mostly softer. Oil <0#CL:> lost 52 cents to $54.43, while the CRB index <.CRB> shed 0.61 percent to 242.35. (Reporting by Wayne Cole)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/BONDS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081121e4bi001ue\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
