\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr bounces, sparks talk of RBA  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2008\par 22:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar pared hefty early losses on Monday as a bounce from major support sparked talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) would not confirm it had acted.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie had sunk to $0.6363 <AUD=> in early trade, from $0.6586 late in New York on Friday, but then quickly jumped to $0.6445, leading dealers to suspect the RBA was buying.\par \par \uc2 * The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least two occasions last week, buying the Aussie around $0.6350 when the market was disorderly and lacking liquidity.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie had come under pressure after the weekend meeting of G20 countries produced a lot of good intentions but no concrete plan to avert a looming global recession [nG7G8].\par \par \uc2 * Instead investors turned risk averse again after Wall Street's <.SPX> late slide on Friday and figures showing a record fall in U.S. retail sales [nN14423234]. Asian shares were seen falling further on Monday.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie also lapsed to 62.49 yen <AUDJPY=R>, from peaks around 64.95 in New York, as safe haven flows lifted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the board.\par \par \uc2 * The same rush to safety benefited bonds. Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> rose 0.105 points to 96.250, while the 10-year contract <YTCc1> added 0.070 to 95.030.\par \par \uc2 * Australian bill futures <0#YBA:> continue to price in a rate cut of at least 75 basis points at the RBA's next policy meeting in December.\par \par \uc2 * Data on third-quarter inflation-adjusted retail sales are due later on Monday and while analysts generally expect a modest 0.5 percent rise, anecdotal evidence is that sales took a severe turn in October.\par \par \uc2 * The minutes of the RBA's November policy meeting are due on Tuesday and analysts are keen to hear why it decided to cut by a larger-than-expected 75 basis points.\par \par \uc2 * RBA Governor Glenn Stevens and RBA Assistant Governor Malcolm Edey both speak on Wednesday. (Reporting by Wayne Cole)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/BONDS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081119e4bg0021g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
