\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Aussie dollar slide pauses as RBA buys time\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wayne Cole   \par \uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2008\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Nov 13 (Reuters) - A battered Australian dollar edged up from deep lows on Thursday after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid an illiquid market and extreme risk-aversion.\par \par \uc2 The local dollar had fallen as far as $0.6347 <AUD=> late in New York on Friday, down over two cents on the day, before edging up to $0.6410 by midday Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The RBA has a good record of stepping in to provide liquidity when it's needed and that's what it has done today," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last stepped in to buy Australian dollars in late October when it plunged as far as $0.6005.\par \par \uc2 "It could easily test 60 cents again," said Rennie. "The volatility in the last 24 hours, particularly on the yen crosses, has been nothing short of intense."\par \par \uc2 The Aussie had tumbled over four yen on Wednesday to bottom around 60.65 yen <AUDJPY=R>, before bouncing just a little to 61.35 on Thursday. The U.S. dollar also slid on the yen as investors fled to the perceived safety of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The local dollar had come under heavy selling pressure after gloomy economic outlooks from chip giant Intel and retailer \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  added to fears of a global recession.\par \par \uc2 As a relatively small open economy, heavily reliant on resource exports, Australia is considered very vulnerable to global slowdowns.\par \par \uc2 Investors also tend to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a play on commodity prices, and the latter have been hit hard this week. The CRB commodity index <.CRB> lost 2.63 percent on Wednesday alone to touch a five-year trough, while oil <0#CL:> dropped over 5 percent to $55.41 a barrel, levels not seen since early 2007.\par \par \uc2 The grim outlook for world growth in turn hit equities, with the S&P 500 <.SPX> dropping over 5 percent and the local market <.AXJO> down 4.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Investors fled stocks for the relative safety of bonds, lifting three-year Australian bond futures to their highest in at least 16 years. Three-year bond futures <YTCc1> jumped 0.170 points to 96.2250, while the 10-year contract climbed 0.160 to 95.070, implying a yield of 4.93 percent.\par \par \uc2 The global gloom only reinforced expectations the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) would cut rates again aggressively in December, if not sooner.\par \par \uc2 The market is now almost fully pricing in a cut of 100 basis points in December, taking rates to 4.25 percent, which would be the lowest since late 2001. Further easing toward 3.5 percent is already priced in for next year.\par \par \uc2 In part, the market is betting inflation will slow more quickly than the RBA currently expects. A survey from the Melbourne Institute on Thursday showed consumers expected consumer price inflation to slow to an annual 3.3 percent over the next 12 months, down from 4.4 percent in October.\par \par \uc2 That was the lowest reading since September last year and a long way from July's peak of 5.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 The fall should be a comfort to the RBA which has been expecting only a very grudging decline in underlying inflation, not reaching its 2 to 3 percent target until the end of 2010. (Reporting by Wayne Cole; Editing by Mark Bendeich)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081116e4bd0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
