\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Dlr Lower Late, Bond Yields Drop To Record Lows\b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2008\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par                            Rates At 0455 GMT \par                            Latest       Change \par  AUD/USD                   0.6392        -3.2% \par  AUD/JPY                   61.09         -5.3% \par  5.75% June. 11, 2011      3.74%         -0.155 \par  6.00% Feb. 15, 2017       4.74%         -0.13 \par  10-Yr Spread To U.S.      +134 bps      -12 bps \par  SFE Dec 3-Year Futures    96.235        +0.18 \par  SFE Dec 10-Year Futures   95.08         +0.17 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar was weaker late Thursday as fear-based trades returned to global financial markets in force, which in turn helped push domestic three-year bond yields to new record lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The rout in risk sentiment prompted the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Australian dollar trade in the offshore session to halt a rapid two-and-a-half U.S. cent slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is the fourth time in the past month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped in to support the unit. Traders said RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen around the US$0.6350 level.\par \par \uc2 The move has helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move up off its low of US$0.6351 although the longer-term outlook for the Australian dollar still remains grim, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 At 0455 GMT, the Australian dollar was trading at US$0.6392, down from US$0.6602 late Wednesday having hit a high of US$0.6452. Against the Japanese yen, it was trading at Y61.09 down from Y64.525.\par \par \uc2 The Australian three-year bond futures contract has forged further into uncharted territory on the accelerating flight-to-safety bid in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The December three-year bond futures contract rose 18 ticks to 96.235 having hit an all-time-high of 96.25. The December 10-year futures contract rose 17 ticks to 95.08.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the physical three-year bond - which has an inverse relationship with the futures contract - touched a record low of 3.735%.\par \par \uc2 The falls in shorter-dated yields has also prompted a continued escalation in the 3-10-year implied bond yield spread to 115 basis points from 111 basis points yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Halmarick, chief economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , acknowledged the rally in the bond market had been substantial, but said that investors were not prepared to call a peak given the aggressive pricing for RBA rate cuts still in the market.\par \par \uc2 "You can't stand in front of the train," Halmarick said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, commodities prices have also declined with general asset prices.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the Australian dollar's relative strength in the face of awful performances by regional equities markets Thursday and economic data suggests a more protracted selloff is not a given.\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Sean Callow said the interest in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at US$0.6350 by both the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and investors was proving significant.\par \par \uc2 "I think you'd have to say the Aussie's performance is very encouraging given that we haven't seen anything that would provide us with any encouragement on the risk side," Callow said.\par \par \uc2 While bigger picture fundamentals for the Australian dollar such as commodities prices, interest rate support and risk appetite continue to crumble, there may be a reluctance to sell too hard or too fast.\par \par \uc2 "Some people would think that the low US$0.60s would be as far as the Aussie gets or that if it goes much beyond that, it wouldn't be for very long," he said.\par \par \uc2 However, ABN AMRO Director of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy Greg Gibbs said that, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks ago has helped support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it remains to be seen whether the latest actions will be enough to prevent further declines in the unit.\par \par \uc2 "It reflects the fact they showed their hand around the US$0.6000s some weeks ago, and they are showing the market they are still there," Gibbs said.\par \par \uc2 Shaking market sentiment were concerns U.S. Treasury's US$700 billion plan to remove troubled assets from banks' balance sheets was in a state of flux.\par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson Wednesday flagged a move to instead support the struggling consumer credit sector.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Sam Holmes, Dow Jones Newswires; \par    61-2-8235-2964; samuel.holmes@dowjones.com (James Glynn in Sydney contributed to this story) \par  \par    (Data provided by Reuters) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 13-11-08 0521GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1054 22765 4109   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081113e4bd0007p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
