\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2008\par 03:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA CONSUMER INFLATION EXPECTATIONS DROP TO 3.3% IN NOV \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian inflationary expectations plummeted in November to their lowest reading since September 2007, reflecting increased gloom about the outlook for the economy and the potential for rising unemployment, according to the Melbourne Institute of Applied Economic and Social Research. (published 0000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: RBA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO BUY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 AUSTRALIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DLR TO ADD LIQUIDITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the Australian dollar, buying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in overnight trading, a spokesman for the bank said Thursday. (published 2318 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA OCTOBER FISCAL SURPLUS CNY118.58 BILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China recorded a fiscal surplus of CNY118.58 billion in October, according to figures issued by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US TSY MCCORMICK SEES SIGNS OF PROGRESS IN HELPING MKTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top U.S. Treasury Department official sees signs of progress in the government's efforts to relieve credit markets. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2NDUPDATE: NZ RETAIL SALES FALL; WEAK TREND WORST IN 13 YRS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand retail sales fell in the third quarter, marking the third consecutive quarterly drop for the first time since the March quarter of 1997 and underlined calls for further aggressive rate cuts to defend the country's recessionary economy. (published 2340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ OCT MANUFACTURING PMI FALLS TO NEW NADIR; ECON HIT HARD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity sunk to new lows in October, suggesting a grim outlook for the sector as the global financial turmoil hurts demand and production, a survey released Thursday showed. (published 0038 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ OCT HOUSE PRICES +1.5% VS SEP, DOWN 4.3% ON YEAR - ASSOC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand home prices ticked up slightly in October, although the number of units sold remained muted, a report released Thursday showed. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA FSC: NO PLAN ON BANKS' SUBORDINATED BONDS PURCHASE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Financial Services Commission said Thursday that there is no plan underway for a joint fund by state-run \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and pension funds to purchase subordinated bonds or redeemable preferred stocks from domestic banks because their average capital adequacy ratio of 10.79% as of the end of September is deemed sound. (published 0150 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA MIN: NO PLAN TO SEEK IMF'S SHORT-TERM LOAN FACILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia has no plan to seek a short-term loan facility from the International Monetary Fund as it "has no problem" with its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves level, a minister said. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE CTRL BK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN LOCAL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MKT - TRADERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday, selling a fairly small amount of U.S. dollars at the opening of trade to prevent the greenback's sharp rise against the peso, traders said. (published 0144 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ: SEC MAY LIMIT CONFLICTS OF INTEREST AT CREDIT-RTG FIRMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Securities and Exchange Commission is expected to vote for rules next week aimed at limiting conflicts of interest at credit-rating firms, but it isn't expected to adopt more substantial changes for the time being. (published 0048 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =WHITE HOUSE SEES COMMON GROUND, BUT EXPECTATIONS LOW FOR G-20 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The White House says world leaders will find common ground when they gather in Washington on Saturday for crisis talks on the global financial meltdown. (published 2225 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: CARRY TRADES A CASUALTY OF THE SASHIMI TRADE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the casualties of the global financial crisis/economic meltdown has been the carry trade, where investors take funds out of low-yielding - but safer - investments and put them in higher-yielding - but riskier - places. (published 0012 GMT) [ 13-11-08 0201GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081113e4bd0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
