\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dollar on defensive despite c.bank support\b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 02:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 __________AUSTRALIAN CREDIT/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (0100 GMT) SNAPSHOT__________ \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (against previous Sydney close) <AUD=> \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0.6050/53 (0.6112/15)   <AUDNZD=R>1.1045/66  (1.1099/08)\par <AUDJPY=R>  56.45/52 (57.00/05) <JPY=>    93.30/34   (93.19/25)\par <AUDEUR=R> 0.4873/78 (0.4872/77) <EUR=>  1.2410/15 \par (1.2515/18)\par  DEBT FUTURES                             CASH YIELDS 90-DAY\par BILL<YBAc1> (DEC) 95.280( 0.000)  <AU3MBB=RR> 5.82(5.80) 3-YR\par BOND  <YTTc1> (DEC) 95.700(-0.020) <AU3YT=RR>   4.29(4.23)\par 10-YR BOND <YTCc1> (DEC) 94.860(-0.060) <AU10YT=RR>  5.14(5.04)\par 3/10 SPREAD  +0.840 (+0.720)   AUST/US 10-YR SPREAD  +143(+137)\par S&P/ASX 200 3743.1 (3809.2)    US10-YR <US10YT=RR>   3.71(3.67)\par __________________________ Oct 28______________________________\par  DATA - NAB survey shows business conditions worsened\par markedly in the third quarter. Its main index falls 10 points\par to -4, the lowest reading since mid-2001 [nSYD73244].\par _______________________________________________________________\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 * The Australian dollar remained on the defensive on Tuesday after steep losses in the past few days which had led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its support for three straight sessions.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie fell as far as $0.6007, its lowest since April 2003, late in New York on Monday as a renewed slide in U.S. shares <.SPX> fuelled a flight to safety.\par \par \uc2 * That led the Reserve bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Aussie dollars, essentially providing liquidity in a defective market where other buyers were almost non-existent.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie bounced as far as $0.6090, but traders reported fresh sell orders around 61 U.S. cents.\par \par \uc2 * Sentiment soured by renewed fall in Tokyo shares, where the Topix index <.TOPX> has lost a massive 24 percent in just the past week, prompting Japanese investors to repatriate funds.\par \par \uc2 * The Aussie was under pressure around 56.50 yen, given the global trend to unwinding leveraged bets made with borrowed yen. It has lost 45 percent against the yen in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 * The yen <JPY=> hovered near 13-year peaks against the U.S. dollar and rose to its highest since May 2002 versus the euro, despite a statement from Group of Seven financial officials expressing concern about the yen's rapid surge [nLR268373].\par \par \uc2 * Australian bond futures eased slightly in line with softer U.S. Treasuries, but were underpinned by safe-haven flows and expectations of a global recession.\par \par \uc2 * Three-year bond futures eased 0.020 points to 95.700, while the 10-year futures contract shed 0.060 points to 94.860. They tailed U.S. Treasuries which fell as the start of a government programme to support the commercial paper market spurred a reversal of safe-haven buying.\par \par \uc2 * Australian bill futures remained firm on expectations the RBA will cut official interest rates again at its monthly policy meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 * Investors are pricing in an easing of at least 50 basis points in the 6.0 percent cash rate. They also expect cuts in the U.S., UK and euro zone in the next week or two.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Wayne Cole; Editing by Mark Bendeich)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-BONDS/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -NOON|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081031e4as0002n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{