\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(10/28) Asian Markets Dive And Europe Follows\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WSJA(10/28) Asian Markets Dive And Europe Follows\par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA)\par \par \uc2 A massive selloff in Asian shares Monday highlights growing investor nervousness over emerging vulnerabilities in a region until recently considered relatively safe from crisis contagion.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo shares fell 6.4% to their lowest level in 25 years and Hong Kong's stock market fell 12.7% as fearful investors pulled out of the region's equity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The Philippines benchmark index in Manila fell 12.3%, while Bangkok tumbled 10.5%. Shanghai fell 6.3% and Singapore lost 8.3%. Taiwan shed 4.7% and Mumbai lost 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Only South Korea defied the trend, closing modestly higher after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a surprise, three-quarter percentage-point rate cut.\par \par \uc2 European markets fell sharply as well Monday. Investors were watching U.S. markets to see whether the pullback would continue as investors shunned risk and moved into cash or the safest of investments.\par \par \uc2 Asia's declines, which come after a punishing day of losses Friday, signal the financial crisis that began with subprime mortgages in the U.S. has moved squarely into the heart of Asia. While exposure among the region's banks to tightening global credit have been one point of concern, the most vulnerable access point, it turns out, is Asia's dependence on exports to the weakened U.S. and European economies.\par \par \uc2 'The false premise all along has been that Asia could decouple,' said Stephen Roach, Asia chairman for \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . 'I think we're seeing in a global recession there is no decoupling from the powerful cross-border linkages of trade flows, capital flows, labor flows and information flows.'\par \par \uc2 Asian-Pacific \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly continued to fall against the U.S. dollar. Though the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday and Monday, the Australian dollar remains near five-year lows. Hong Kong monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seen as a haven, from rising too much against the U.S. dollar, to which it is pegged.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average lost 486.18 points to 7162.90 as nervous investors sold shares of banks and exporters on fresh concerns over the fallout from the U.S. financial crisis and an ever-strengthening yen.\par \par \uc2 Gloom mounted over Japan's banks Monday after Mitsubishi UFJ, Japan's largest lender, said it would raise as much as 990 billion yen, or $10.7 billion, in capital through share issues within the next year. Until recently, Japan's banks appeared to be among the world's strongest.\par \par \uc2 The surging yen prompted finance chiefs from the Group of Seven leading nations to warn that recent 'excessive volatility' in the yen threatens the global economy and financial system.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei is now at its lowest level since October 1982, when Japan was on the eve of an export-led boom that culminated in the burst of its stock-and-real-estate bubble in the early 1990s.\par \par \uc2 'It's a crisis. It's a psychological shock to think we're back to where we were almost 30 years ago' said Hisashi Takeda, a real-estate worker in central Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is heavily dependent on exports for growth, making it vulnerable to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations and economic downturns in overseas markets. In 2007, exports were responsible for 67% of Japan's GDP growth.\par \par \uc2 'More than half of growth comes from exports,' says Akira Maekawa, a senior economist for UBS Securities Japan.\par \par \uc2 In addition to concerns about exports, other fears have dealt a blow to Asian markets this month. Losses from leveraged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions revealed by \uc2 Citic Pacific Ltd\uc2 ., a Hong Kong-listed conglomerated backed by Chinese state money, have made investors nervous about other similar undisclosed liabilities on corporate balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 Another trigger: the president of Argentina's decision to nationalize the country's pension funds, which raised fears that other small nations under pressure from the financial crisis might resort to extreme measures to meet their obligations. 'For the first time in a long time, we started hearing people talk about 'emerging-market contagion,'' said Steve Diggle, partner at Artradis Fund Management, a US$4 billion hedge fund in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 - -\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020081028e4as000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
