\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Australia \highlight\uc2  cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  again to buy A$\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 00:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst quote, background)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Oct 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the Aussie dollar for a third straight day on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to fresh 5-\u189\'00\'BD year lows against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore trade.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was providing liquidity in an illiquid market, echoing comments made a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 Dealers also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying the Australian dollar at around the $0.6060 level during the European session on Monday, but the RBA declined to confirm that.\par \par \uc2 "They are trying to make the decline less disorderly," said John Horner, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "Despite their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the process of deleveraging and pricing of a global slowdown will continue to weigh down on the Aussie."\par \par \uc2 The Aussie has tumbled in recent months as investors unwound a five-year long boom in carry trades -- where they borrowed in cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and Swiss franc to buy high-yielding Australian assets.\par \par \uc2 Falling commodity prices, amid fears of a global recession, have only added to the Aussie's woes, given that the country is a large exporter of natural resources.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie <AUD=> was at $0.6025, off a trough of $0.6007 struck in offshore trade, its lowest since April 2003. It has lost more than 38 percent since peaking in mid-July as investors shunned high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of extreme risk aversion that afflicted global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was at 56.12 yen, not far from 55.11 yen <AUDJPY=R> struck late last week, which was its lowest since it was floated in 1983. The Aussie has lost about 46 percent against the yen in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to spend large amounts shoring up the beleaguered Aussie. The RBA has relatively modest official reserves of A$36 billion ($21.6 billion), including A$33 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Japan has almost a trillion U.S. dollars in reserves, South Korea around $240 billion and Thailand $103 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly from September 2000 to September 2001, buying a net A$3.6 billion over that period, when the local dollar fell as far as 49 U.S. cents. Later, it made a healthy profit on those purchases by selling the Australian dollar above 80 and 90 U.S. cents. (Reporting by Anirban Nag; Editing by James Thornhill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|AUF|J|ABN|E|U|C|D|M|O|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|ABX|BNX|FUN|DNP|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081030e4ar002mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
