\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4- \highlight22\uc2 Australia \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to buoy ailing Aussie\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds funds industry in talks with government)\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Oct 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a second day on Monday to prop up the tumbling Australian dollar after investors hammered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-year lows in the face of the global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) said the action was aimed at boosting liquidity in the market. It did not reveal how much it had spent buying the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday and Monday, or at what level it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in August 2007, when the U.S. subprime mortgage meltdown first set the global credit crisis in motion.\par \par \uc2 "We did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while on Monday morning as the activity was similar to that on Friday," a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 By 4.15 p.m. (0515 GMT), the Australian dollar was at $0.6115, not far from a five-and-a-half low of $0.6055 on Friday, and at 57.29 yen, up from Friday's 55.11 on the yen -- its lowest level against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it was first floated in 1983.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as the Aussie, has fallen about 45 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 38 percent against the U.S. dollar since peaking three months ago.\par \par \uc2 The yen surged last week, hitting a 13 year high against the dollar and a six-year high against the euro as investors cut overseas investments funded in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and repatriated funds. At one point on Friday, the euro dropped more than 10 percent against the yen.\par \par \uc2 That culminated on Monday with a warning by the Group of Seven rich nations that the yen's wild swings threatened financial stability, fanning speculation other central banks may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the rally in the Japanese unit.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie has tumbled as investors unwound a five-year long boom in carry trades -- where investors borrowed in cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and Swiss franc to buy high-yielding Australian assets like local government debt.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, Australian interest rates were among the highest in the developed world.\par \par \uc2 But earlier this month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates by 100 basis points in one go, taking them to 6 percent and eroding some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yield advantage.\par \par \uc2 The cut ushered in a globally coordinated series of rate cuts by major central banks hoping to bolster confidence in the world's shattered financial markets.\par \par \uc2 More RBA rate cuts are expected next month and in December.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The RBA has a good history of providing liquidity during periods of erratic price movements in the Aussie dollar and I do not see the last action as more than that," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 Falling commodity prices, amid fears of a global recession, have added to the Aussie's woes, given that a large portion of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings come from sales of raw materials like coal, iron ore and wheat.\par \par \uc2 However, Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan said the decline in the Australian dollar could bring some benefits to the economy, providing some relief to exporters and the tourism industry.\par \par \uc2 That would be a help as signs point to a slowdown in the economy, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "It's generally positive for companies because a lot of our exports are priced in U.S. dollars," said Eric Betts, equities strategist, \uc2 Nomura Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "So it will offset some of the impact of the commodity price slump. It will also help the manufacturers because they compete with imports. It will also help inbound tourism."\par \par \uc2 Exporters such as \uc2 CSL Ltd\uc2 , \uc2 Foster's Group Ltd\uc2  and \uc2 Amcor Ltd\uc2  could benefit when they convert overseas earnings into Aussie dollars.\par \par \uc2 That could provide an extra cushion for the economy on top of measures already announced by the government, including a stimulus package to help households and a guarantee to shield local bank deposits from the credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 The deposit guarantee, however, has thrown the savings industry into crisis as investors fled managed funds and foreign banks for the relative safety of domestic banks.\par \par \uc2 Some A$15 billion ($9.3 billion) in investor funds is now frozen, research firm Morningstar says.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Colonial First State Global Asset Management, a division of the \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , became the latest to suspend withdrawals from some of its mortgage funds, which have assets of A$3.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Perpetual, Axa Asia Pacific and Everest \uc2 Babcock & Brown\uc2 , suspended redemptions in mortgage and income funds last week.\par \par \uc2 The fund management industry held talks with the government on Monday to discuss ways to stem the flow of cash out of the industry, though the government has ruled out extending the deposit guarantee to market-linked funds. (Additional reporting by Denny Thomas, Sonali Paul, Mette Fraende and Victoria Thieberger) (Editing by Jonathan Standing and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/AUSTRALIA (UPDATE 4)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081030e4ar000t5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
