\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Mixed, Volatile; Watch For US Cues\b0\par\par\uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 02:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates levels)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets are volatile Monday and caution remains the order of the day after large falls in the region over the past week.\par \par \uc2 Korea's Kospi Composite is up 0.8%, supported by a surprise interest rate cut by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; it rose 1.9% at the open but then fell as much as 2.6%, before the rate cut was announced.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 is down 1.8% after an early, brief rise; Australia's S&P/ASX 200 has fallen 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's market recently opened, with the main index dropping 5.5%.\par \par \uc2 Stocks are struggling to find some measure of stability after the large falls already registered in many bourses over the past week; the Nikkei 225 fell 12% last week, the Kospi 20.5%, the S&P/ASX 200 2.6% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index 13.3%.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures were higher in screen trade, although few would put much store in those moves for now; S&P 500 futures were quoted up 0.5%, Nasdaq 100 futures up 1.5% and DJIA futures up 119 points.\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps the most accurate statement of the day for equity markets is that we are now closer to the bottom than we were last week," said Sonray Chief Economist Clifford Bennett.\par \par \uc2 Either way, volatility was expected to persist as economic concerns quickly replaced financial market worries as the predominant issue for investors as it became clear the slowdown was global in nature and perhaps more severe than initially thought.\par \par \uc2 The coming week would bring the next Federal Reserve rate meeting in the U.S., with markets expecting a further rate cut there, while there were key earnings reports due from large-cap companies in the financial and technology space in both the U.S. and Asia.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economists said Asian economies looked "far better today" in terms of short-term debt and reserves and current account balances but if market confidence continued to deteriorate "the 'multiple equilibria' problems of 1997 could re-emerge, although hopefully less virulently given the better fundamentals today."\par \par \uc2 "We continue to see the risks to our already below-consensus forecasts for Asia ex-Japan as very much to the downside, and remain cautious about the outlook for equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region," they said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese bank stocks were falling on various media reports that banks were considering capital boosts; \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  was down 14%, \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group\uc2  down 13% and \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\uc2  ask-only at Y410,000. According to Tokyo Stock Exchange rules, when the flow of share orders would trigger an automatic suspension if executed, a special ask-only or bid-only price is indicated, even though shares do not actually trade.\par \par \uc2 Korean markets were trading off the outcome of the unscheduled Bank of Korea meeting, where rates were cut 75 basis points; still, "as investors seem to almost ignore valuations when trading these days, it's not easy to predict a bottom for the Kospi," said Kim Seung-Han, from Hi Investment & Securities. Financial stocks were supported with \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  up 5.9%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were jumpy, with the yen still well-bid on risk aversion, although the U.S. dollar and euro were off their early lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance minister Shoichi Nakagawa warned authorities there were closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were bad for the economy and financial markets, a sign that officials were growing more concerned about the pace of the yen gains.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was recently around Y93.80 after falling to Y91.91 earlier, still down from Y94.69 late in New York on Friday; the euro was at Y118.20 after falling to Y115.33. The euro was around US$1.2599, compared to US$1.2609 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Investor risk aversion would spur further repatriation into the U.S. dollar and yen by American and Japanese investors as well as safe haven inflows by foreign investors, said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 UBS Investment Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This is a driver that is likely to continue to year end as real money managers cut their losses in overseas assets, emerging markets and commodities before they close their 2008 books," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was trading around US$0.6195 with market sources saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there bought at US$0.6125 in very thin trade; the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed earlier Monday that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Australian dollar in Europe on Friday as it plunged by seven U.S. cents to a five-year low of US$0.6057.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures were taking their lead from stock markets with lead December futures now up 0.10 at 137.80 on the Nikkei decline.\par \par \uc2 Base metals remained under pressure with "the bearish sentiment justified based on the recent macro economic data prints in Europe and the US," said analysts at Standard Bank; three-month LME copper was down $260 from the PM kerb at $3,770 a ton.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $734 per troy ounce, down 30 cents from New York; \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  analyst David Moore though was tipping the metal to track higher over the week as the liquidation of gold positions in the flight-to-cash trend seemed to be abated.\par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude was down 42 cents at US$63.73 per barrel on Globex after falling US$3.69 in New York on Friday; oil was still under pressure despite news late Friday the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries would cut output, as OPEC's action was viewed as insufficient.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 27-10-08 0105GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081027e4ar0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
