\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie near record lows vs yen; gets c.bank support\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2008\par 22:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie mired near multi-year lows despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for second straight day on Monday\par \par \uc2 * G7 issues statement expressing concerns about yen's surge\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar hovered near record lows against the yen and 5-\u189\'00\'BD year troughs on the U.S. dollar on Monday, failing to get much respite from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Aussie dollars for a second day on Monday, after first stepping in on Friday, as the growing risk of a global recession continued to see investors exit positions in higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August 2007 and its latest move sparked renewed speculation that other central banks would follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Adding to growing talk of a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks was a statement from the Group of Seven big industrialised economies. The G7 said on Monday that a rapid rise in the yen would prove harmful for both the economy and markets and they would cooperate.\par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> rose across the board, trading very near 13-year highs against the U.S. dollar as safe-haven inflows gathered momentum on the back of tumbling stock markets.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan is the next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the RBA that one would expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from given the negative impact that a surging yen has on exporters and its stock markets," said Tony Morriss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ.\par \par \uc2 By 4:20 p.m. the Aussie <AUD=> was at $0.6112 against the U.S. dollar, having fallen to $0.6055 in offshore trade, its lowest since April 2003. The Aussie had been at $0.6510 late in local trade on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the Aussie was at 57 yen, having fallen as far as 55.11 yen <AUDJPY=R> offshore, its lowest since it was floated in 1983. The fall brought the Aussie's losses against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last three months to 85 percent.\par \par \uc2 Commodities were also weak, hurting the Aussie further. Australia is a major exporter of resources so recent steep falls in commodity prices augur ill for the economy, particularly as demand in Asia, notably China, is slowing.\par \par \uc2 "Greater concerns about the global growth outlook dampen demand for the Aussie as it is often considered a "proxy" for world growth," said Besa Deda, chief economist at St George. "The growing worries about the global economic outlook and the Chinese economy have spurred selling in commodity prices. This slowing has also pushed the Aussie lower."\par \par \uc2 Pessimism about global economic growth continued to weigh down on investors. The UK economy shrank 0.5 percent in the third-quarter while Europe's economy also looks to be headed into recession, backing a view that the downturn in the United States was adversely impacting other major economies. [nLO103381]\par \par \uc2 Australian bill futures rose <0#YBA:> as investors continued to price aggressive rate cuts by RBA to cushion the economy from the market turmoil. Markets are pricing in a 50 basis point cut next month, following the RBA's shock 100 basis point reduction earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> were indicated 0.005 points higher at 95.75, while the 10-year futures contract <YTCc1> shed 0.115 points to 94.96. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ----------------(Snapshot at\par 4:10p.m./0510MT)------------------ FUTURES                     \par           CASH YIELD 90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (DEC) 95.240(+0.060)\par  <AU3MBB=RR>  5.80 (5.89) 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1> (DEC)\par 95.750(+0.005)   <AU3YT=RR>  4.23 (4.29) 10-YR BOND <YTCc1>\par (DEC) 94.960(-0.115)   <AU10YT=RR> 5.04 (4.96) AUD/USD <AUD=>\par 0.6112 (0.6510) US 10-YR   <US10YT=RR> 3.67 (3.61)\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par  AUD VS   2-YR          10-YR            *AUD 3-YR/10-YR SPREAD\par USD      +254 (+252)    +137 (+134)    *FUTURES +0.790 (+0.660)\par CAD      +192 (+194)    +140 (+132)      *AUD 2-YR/10-YR SPREAD\par NZD      -159 (-132)     -88 ( -97)      *CASH     +102 ( +90)\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by James Thornhill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/BONDS (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081029e4aq002kz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
