\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST-Australian Business News - Sep 13   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2007\par 23:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Compiled for Reuters by Media Monitors)   \par \par \uc2 THE AUSTRALIAN FINANCIAL REVIEW (  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.afr.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.afr.com  }}}\uc2 )   \par \par \uc2 Just Group yesterday said it would continue its expansion of the Jay Jays jeans brand into South Africa and launch its Peter Alexander sleepwear brand in the United States. The fashion retailer reported a 7.4 percent rise in net profit to A$61.4 million for 2006-07, once one-offs were excluded. Analysts are now speculating on whether Melbourne billionaire, Solomon Lew, will make a takeover offer. Mr Lew has a 21 percent stake in Just Group through private company, Metrepark. Page 17.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Healthscope and Symbion Health yesterday held talks on an alternative plan after their A$2.8 billion merger was defeated by Primary Health Care, which holds a 20 percent stake in Symbion. Healthscope managing director, Bruce Dixon, said possible measures included a reverse takeover, joint ventures and an asset sale, which would require a majority of investor support, rather than the 75 percent required at Tuesday's vote. Analysts said they expected Primary to respond after its two rival healthcare providers announced their plan B strategy by next week. Page 17.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto has increased pressure on the Mongolian Government to ratify an investment agreement for the Oyu Tolgoi copper-gold project after the issue of a US$350 million credit facility to Rio's joint venture partner, Ivanhoe Mines. The funds will be used to ensure the development schedule of first production by early 2010. Because there has been no progress on the June draft agreement, Ivanhoe has threatened to suspend work, while Rio says it might scale down its rate of investment in the project. Page 18.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 German discounter, Aldi, plans to expand store numbers to 650 across Australia in a bid to challenge the dominant market position of rival supermarket operators, Coles Group and Woolworths. Managing director, Michael Kloeters, said there was potential for Aldi to increase store numbers in New South Wales, Queensland and Victoria to 500, and open 150 new outlets across South Australia and Western Australia. Aldi, which launched its Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early 2001, yesterday opened its 150th store, in the Melbourne suburb of St Kilda. Page 18.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 THE AUSTRALIAN (  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.theaustralian.news.com.au  }}}\uc2 )   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) has defended its decision not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading of the Australian dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a A$1.4 billion loss in the financial year despite adding A$4.9 billion to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock. Governor, Glenn Stevens, said the bank saw no reason to actively trade to protect the value of foreign assets because the dollar's rise was consistent with the strength of the domestic economy. The loss was the RBA's first since the 1993-94 fiscal year. Page 19.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 A report by the World Bank supports claims by Federal Treasury secretary, Ken Henry, that Australia could shield itself from the global effects of a slowdown in the United States economy because of its strong sales to China. The bank's senior economist in Beijing, Louis Kuijs, said China's gross domestic product had been growing fast but 'there's still no obvious over-heating in the economy'. He also said China's inflation was not out of step with higher food prices throughout the world. China is now Australia's biggest trading partner. Page 19.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 South Australian Premier, Mike Rann, yesterday said the State Government could lift the 15 percent cap on individual shareholdings in Santos , but would want the oil and gas explorer to maintain its headquarters in the state. 'If I'm going to take a bill into the parliament to lift the cap...I've got to demonstrate to South Australians there is a dividend in it for them,' Mr Rann said. In May, the Government began an investigation of Santos's application to have the cap removed, and has promised to make a decision by the end of this month. Page 20.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Federal Small Business Minister, Fran Bailey, has joined an Australian Chamber of Commerce and Industry campaign against calls for the removal of small business owners' exemption from the Privacy Act. 'The compliance burden on small business would far outweigh any wider benefit if the exemption was removed,' Ms Bailey said. The idea, which would cost business owners about A$3500 per year, was one of 301 recommendations on privacy made in an Australian Law Reform Commission discussion paper issued yesterday. Page 21.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 THE SYDNEY MORNING HERALD (  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.smh.com.au"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.smh.com.au  }}}\uc2 )   \par \par \uc2 Qantas Airways could bolster its new stand-alone freight arm after trucking magnate, Lindsay Fox, and his son, Peter, indicated they would soon sell their majority stake in the Linfox logistics business to a partner. Qantas chief executive, Geoff Dixon, said on Monday that Qantas Freight Enterprises, which was already operating under a new corporate structure, was seeking acquisitions ahead of its float on the Australian Stock Exchange in the next 18 months. Analysts estimated the Fox family's Linfox stake to be worth about A$1 billion. Page 27.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Fairfax Media chief executive, David Kirk, yesterday said that apart from 'small, single-digit-million-type acquisitions' the diversified media company was not considering any further media deals. 'We've got what we want...It's very much an organic growth agenda for Fairfax now,' Mr Kirk said. The newspaper publisher, which plans to expand into radio and television, has spent more than A$4.5 billion over the past two years, including A$2.8 billion on a merger with Rural Press and A$480 million for Southern Cross Broadcasting's radio and production assets. Page 29.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Wesfarmers yesterday announced it had already created a team of top executives to develop a strategy for fusing its Bunnings hardware stores and the newly acquired Coles Group . The West-Australian conglomerate plans to run Bunnings with Coles' stationery business, Officeworks, leave Coles' Target general merchandise business as a single division, and keep Coles' supermarkets alongside petrol stations and bottle shops, but may put Coles' Kmart general merchandise business to auction. Coles shareholders will vote on the takeover in November. Page 29.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 The Australian stockmarket followed a positive lead from Wall Street to reach early morning highs, but retreated throughout the day to close in negative territory. The benchmark S&P/ASX200 fell 15.2 points to 6220.3, while the All Ordinaries finished 12.6 points lower at 6236. CMC Markets analyst, David Land, said major banks coming off their earlier highs weighed heavily on the rest of the market. Mining stocks were stronger, but media stocks were weaker, while the retail and energy sectors were mixed. Page 31.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 THE AGE (  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.theage.com.au"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.theage.com.au  }}}\uc2 )   \par \par \uc2 PBL Media has strengthened its position as Australia's dominant magazine owner with the A$94 million purchase of British publisher Emap's Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Through the acquisition, the company's ACP Magazines division will inherit titles such as New Woman, movie magazine, Empire, and men's magazines, FHM and ZOO Weekly. The deal marks further consolidation in the domestic magazine industry, after News Ltd purchased Federal Publishing Company last November, and Pacific Magazines acquired Time Inc's titles two months ago. Page B3.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Rabobank says the Australian wine industry should innovate or risk being pigeon-holed as a producer of 'cheap-and-cheerful' wines. In its report, the agribusiness banking specialist said Australian producers needed to re-establish their good reputation in top-shelf categories, with demand for Australian premium wines having shrunk since the wine glut which began in 2004. The Australian Wine and Brandy Corporation said the report complemented its plan to establish distinct categories of Australian wine and market them as unique regional products. Page B4.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Shares in big grain companies, AWB , ABB Grain and GrainCorp , are falling amid investor concerns that earnings will suffer as a result of a smaller 2007-08 harvest, despite record world prices. Agribusiness bank, Rabobank, this week estimated the wheat crop would be 15-to-19 tonnes, in line with a United States forecast for a drop in Australia's wheat crop to 18 millions tonnes. The Australian Bureau of Agricultural and Resources Economics, which currently estimates a wheat crop of 22-to-24 million tonnes, will release its latest estimate next week. Page B4.   \par \par \uc2 - - - -   \par \par \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group (ANZ) chief executive, John McFarlane, yesterday said achieving the top ranking globally in the Dow Jones Sustainability Index put the bank 'in good stead for the future.' The index assesses companies across a range of economic, social and environmental criteria, including corporate governance, workplace practices and environmental policies. The Australian Conservation Foundation said ANZ could improve its environmental credentials by severing ties with a number of south-east Asian logging companies. Page B4.   \par \par \uc2 DIGEST-AUSTRALIA-BUSINESS|LANGEN|AUF|G|RBN|REVU|RNP|DNP|PGE|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070912e39c002fy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
