\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dollar pops above 80c; bonds decline   \b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2007\par 00:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---------------(Snapshot at 8.21 a.m./2221 GMT)----------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (vs Late Sydney)      DEBT FUTURES (Night Session) <AUD=>\par  0.7985 (0.7810)  90-DAY <YBAc1> (SEP) 93.26 (93.27)\par <AUDEUR=R> 0.5924 (0.5820)  3-YR   <YTTc1> (SEP) 93.880(93.930)\par <AUDJPY=R>  91.30 (87.81)   10-YR  <YTCc1> (SEP) 94.135(94.195)\par <AUDNZD=R> 1.1450 (1.1648)  US 10-YR <US10YT=RR> 4.69  (4.62) \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par AU$S/Term range *Support *Restance *RSI-14 *MA-10  *MA-20     \par   *0.7800/8610  *0.7940  *0.8010   *16.05  *0.8297 *0.8460   \par --------------------------(Aug 20)-----------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * AUD offshore range roughly $0.7703/7980.   \f28 \par \par \uc2  * The Aussie dollar <AUD=> briefly rose back above 80 U.S. cents on Monday after rebounding sharply in offshore trade after the Federal Reserve lowered its discount rate on loans to banks in response to the global squeeze in credit markets.   \par \par \uc2  * The Aussie had hit a 10-month low of $0.7670 on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  * Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Aussie also recovered lost ground and was back above 91 yen. The Aussie/yen cross <AUDJPY=R> had dropped to a 14-month low of 85.95 yen on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  * The lows in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hit even after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore trade on Thursday to restore liquidity for the Australian dollar.   \par \par \uc2  * Although the Fed kept its benchmark rate steady at 5.25 percent, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it stood ready to act to keep the U.S. economy on an even keel. Many economists interpreted those comments as a signal the Fed could cut its main rate soon.   \par \par \uc2  * Reuters/Jefferies CRB Index <.CRB> up 1.6 percent on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  * U.S. oil futures <CLU7> rose 1.4 percent to $71.98/barrel.   \par \par \uc2  * Base metals bounced on the London Metals Exchange   \par \par \uc2  * Spot gold <XAU=> was quoted at $656.50/657.30 in early Asian trade versus $655.80/656.60 in New York late on Friday, when it was up from $650.50/651.10 in the previous session.   \par \par \uc2  * Australian bond futures reversed gains in offshore trade after the Fed cut its discount rates and investors focused again on the risks of tighter monetary policy in Australia.   \par \par \uc2  * RBA Governor Glenn Stevens on Friday played down the threat of the turmoil in financial markets to the domestic economy and said he remained concern about domestic inflation accelerating.   \par \par \uc2  * The three-year bond futures <YTTc1> contract fell 0.05 points to 93.880 for a yield of 6.12 percent. The 10-year contract <YTCc1> declined 0.06 points to 94.135, a yield of 5.865 percent.      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/BONDS|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070822e38j001u0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
