\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Australia Dlr Slumps; Largest Weekly Fall Since 1983   \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2007\par 09:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds new prices, new comment)   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    \par  \par                              Rates At 0713 GMT \par                              Latest       Change \par    AUD/USD                   0.7692       -4.5% \par    AUD/JPY                   86.60        -7.3% \par    5.25% Aug 2010            6.13%        -5 bps \par    6.00% Feb. 15, 2017       5.805%       -6 bps \par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.      +125 bps     -2 bps \par    SFE Mar 3-Year Futures    93.915       +0.12 \par    SFE Mar 10-Year Futures   94.185       +0.085 \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar continued to flounder Friday after suffering its largest weekly fall since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1983, as global equities markets continued to fall.   \par \par \uc2 Continued risk aversion, which has led investors to unwind carry trades, pushed the Australian dollar below US$0.7700 late Friday due to a fresh wave of selling out of Europe.   \par \par \uc2 At 0713 GMT, the Australian unit was at US$0.7692, its lowest point since early March and well down from US$0.8056 late Thursday. Against the Japanese yen, the Australian dollar was at Y86.08, down from Y93.515.   \par \par \uc2 Bond futures were higher and yields dropped as investors continued to seek out safe-haven investments.   \par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Besa Deda said liquidity remained very thin, accentuating moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "There's probably not a lot of support in this sort of environment where markets are thin and whippy and investors are really quite nervous," Deda said   \par \par \uc2 National Australia Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos said traders were still finding it hard to see a bottom in the Australian dollar's spiral.   \par \par \uc2 "We know that the average range for the year is about US$0.12 - we just happened to do that in three weeks," he said. "So obviously volatility has gone up significantly."   \par \par \uc2 A rise in volatility can force some investors to reduce their risk, further exacerbating a fall in an asset price.   \par \par \uc2 The question now is how long it will take for markets to reprice the risks related to the U.S. credit crunch that has sent markets into a nosedive in recent weeks.   \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar has tumbled from US$0.85 a week ago to current levels, a slide of nearly 10%, its worst fall since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in December 1983.   \par \par \uc2 The plunge over the last two days prompted the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restore liquidity to the Australian dollar market overnight, the first time it had done so in six years.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts believe further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible.   \par \par \uc2 RBA Governor Glenn Stevens said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought a small amount of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which one analyst estimated to be "a couple hundred" million, Australian dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking before an Australian parliamentary committee Friday, Stevens reaffirmed the RBA's tightening bias despite the recent market woes.   \par \par \uc2 Stevens said while credit market developments added uncertainty to the economic outlook, forecasts for growth remained near trend levels with Australian inflation was likely to remain near the top of the RBA's 2%-3% annual target range.   \par \par \uc2 The RBA raised its cash rate target by 25 basis points to 6.50% last week.   \par \par \uc2 Expectations for another rate rise some time in the next year haven't shifted, however economists said the governor's statements seemed to rule out the chance of a rate hike in September.   \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubting the robust and resilient Australian economy but the global uncertainty suggests that the RBA will stay sidelined for now,"  \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  senior economist Su-Lin Ong said.   \par \par \uc2 (Data provided by Reuters)   \par \par \uc2 -By Sam Holmes, Dow Jones Newswires;   \par \par \uc2 61-2-8235-2964; samuel.holmes@dowjones.com [ 17-08-07 0751GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1054 22765 4109   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070817e38h000b2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
