\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2007\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE:ASIA-PACIFIC CENTRAL BANKS SEEK TO KEEP MARKETS LIQUID    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan pumped more cash into the money market Friday while Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it's ready to inject liquidity again, underscoring that Asia-Pacific central banks remain determined to fight off the credit crisis engulfing global financial markets. (published 0332 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:AUSTRALIA RBA SUPPORTS AUD, FIRST TIME IN SIX YRS    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confirmed Friday that it \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets overnight for the first time in six years, to restore liquidity to Australian dollar trading. (published 0250 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =3RD UPDATE:AUSTRALIA RBA FLAGS OUTLOOK RISKS, BUT BIAS TIGHT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor, Glenn Stevens, said Friday that recent financial market volatility added uncertainty to the economic outlook, but he remains concerned about domestic inflation pressures as demand surges across the economy. (published 0413 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBA GOV: AUSTRALIAN BANKS HAVE DIVERSIFIED FUNDING BASE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia Governor Glenn Stevens said Friday he has no regrets about last week's interest rate hike, but is keeping a careful eye on the current market turmoil in deciding the future track for monetary policy. (published 0046 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:AUSTRALIA'S COSTELLO SAYS NO REASON FOR BANKS TO HIKE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Peter Costello said Friday there's no reason for Australia's major banks to raise their interest rates, despite the increased cost of borrowing stemming from the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis. (published 0359 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA PBOC SELLS CNY101B 3-YR BONDS IN SPECIAL SALE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold CNY101 billion worth of three-year bonds in a special sale Friday to control liquidity, it said in a statement. (published 0358 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF SHINOHARA: CLOSELY WATCHING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  point man said Friday he will closely watch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves after the yen surged sharply overnight in New York. (published 0030 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:PHILIPPINE BUDGET IN SURPLUS JUL,BACK ON FISCAL TRACK    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines posted a budget surplus of PHP1.6 billion in July, with revenue agencies overshooting collection targets to put the government back on track to hit its fiscal goals for the year. (published 0451 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN JULY DEPARTMENT STORE SALES DOWN 4.3% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's nationwide department store sales in July fell 4.3% on year to Y722.7 billion, adjusted for change in the number of stores, the Japan Department Stores Association said Friday. (published 0543 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP:SINGAPORE ADJUSTED JUL NON-OIL EXPORTS +0.5% ON MO    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's mainstay exports rose less than expected in July as another month of weak electronics shipments offset healthy growth in the chemical and drug industries, government figures showed Friday. (published 0500 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA JUNE MANUFACTURING SALES UP 2.6% ON YR, +3.2% ON MO    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's manufacturing sales rose 2.6% in June from a year earlier to MYR43.78 billion, the Statistics Department said Friday. (published 0401 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI BOT: BAHT'S RECENT SOFTNESS WON'T LAST INDEFINITELY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The baht's recent softness isn't likely to be sustained so exporters should be cautious about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk and continue to manage such risk, the Bank of Thailand Governor Tarisa Watanagase said Friday. (published 0513 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ========== \par  \par  \par =BOJ WATCH: THURS RATE HIKE CHANCES FADING FAST ON MKT TURMOIL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chance that the Bank of Japan will raise interest rates next week, once considered a near-certainty, is now falling as fast as the Tokyo stock market. (published 0533 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS:DLR/YEN VOLATILITIES SURGE OVER 20%;MAY TOP 40%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volatilities implied by dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options surged over 20.0% in Tokyo Friday and may continue rising as global financial unrest stemming from U.S. subprime loan jitters is spurring demand for options. (published 0534 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(8/17) BREAKINGVIEWS: AVOIDING CREDIT TURMOIL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE RATINGS agencies are the whipping boys for the credit crunch. They certainly deserve some of the blame. But what sort of punishment? The reaction of politicians will be to pile on the red tape. Barney Frank, chairman of the U.S. House of Representatives' Financial Services Committee, and Charlie McCreevy, the European Union's internal-market commissioner, are limbering up for a fight. (published 0432 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(8/17) CREDIT MARKETS: NEW VILLAIN IN MARKET DRAMA: CP    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commercial paper, the usually humdrum market for short-term debt held by money-market funds and used by companies to finance their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has suddenly taken center stage in the credit crunch. (published 0313 GMT) [ 17-08-07 0600GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070817e38h00067\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
