\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dollar tumbles, RBA acts to cushion fall   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2007\par 06:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Aug 17 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar fell 3 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and over 6 percent against the Japanese yen on Friday, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as risk aversion enveloped global markets.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight for the first time since 2001 to restore liquidity for the Australian dollar, which had fallen to a fresh five-month low against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Governor Glenn Stevens said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was small and added the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more to ensure orderly movements in the Australian dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "They \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide liquidity but in an environment where risk aversion is so high, there is bound to be downward pressure on the Aussie," said Besa Deda, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commonwealth Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Falling risk appetite has led to an escalation in investors unwinding carry trades. Under these trades, investors borrow in the low-yielding Japanese yen to buy higher-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Aussie and New Zealand dollars. Their past popularity has been a prime reason for the local unit's recent rise.   \par \par \uc2 The Aussie slumped to its lowest since Sept. 2006 against the Japanese yen, falling as low as 87.45 yen <AUDJPY=R>, down over 22 percent since its July 20, 16-year high of 107.72 yen.   \par \par \uc2 At 4:00 p.m. (0600 GMT), the Aussie <AUD=> was at $0.7810/15, having fallen as far as $0.7781 in early deals, its weakest since March 9, and well down on Thursday's $0.8051/56. It has fallen over 12 percent since it hit a 18-year peak of $0.8871 on July 25.   \par \par \uc2 STEVENS SPEAKS   \par \par \uc2 Since then, the Aussie has been on a downward spiral as the contagion from the deepening woes in the credit market led to sharp falls in stocks and forced investors to cut exposure to riskier assets.   \par \par \uc2 RBA Governor Stevens played down the threat of global market turmoil to the domestic economy. But he said he was less comfortable with the speed and extent of the market adjustment, which he said was "bordering on the irrational" at times.   \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt that a tightening bias remains in the conduct of policy but the notable reference on a number of occasions throughout the testimony to uncertainty surrounding the current global credit situation, including an acknowledgement that it may get worse, suggests that the RBA is sidelined for now," said Su-Lin Ong, senior economist at  \uc2 RBC Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted interest rates to a decade high of 6.5 percent and on Monday it raised its inflation forecast for the year, leaving the door open for more hikes.   \par \par \uc2 But deepening troubles in the U.S. subprime mortgage markets and a growing investor preference for safe-haven assets have bolstered government bonds, and Aussie bill futures are now pricing in less chance of a rate hike in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 The 10-year contract <YTCc1> extended gains to advance by 0.085 points to 94.195, its highest since March 23. Australian three-year bond futures <YTTc1> jumped 0.13 points to 93.93, their highest since early May.   \par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday once again pumped in a large amount of funds into the banking system to boost liquidity. In its regular daily money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added A$3.87 billion ($3.07 billion) in cash, above a usual daily average of around A$1.8 billion.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------(Snapshot at 4:30 p.m./0630 GMT)----------------\par FUTURES                                   CASH YIELD     \par 90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (SEP) 93.270(+0.020) <AU3MBB=RR> 6.81 (6.86)\par 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1> (SEP) 93.930(+0.130) <AU3YT=RR>  6.10 (6.21)\par 10-YR BOND <YTCc1> (SEP) 94.195(+0.085) <AU10YT=RR> 5.81 (5.88)\par AUD/USD <AUD=> 0.7805 (0.8045) US 10-YR <US10YT=RR> 4.62 (4.70)\par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par AUD VS   2-YR           10-YR           *AUD 3-YR/10-YR SPREAD\par USD      +205 (+198)    +118 (+118)     *FUTURES   -26.5\par (-31.0) CAD      +200 (+197)    +138 (+145)     *AUD 2-YR/10-YR\par SPREAD NZD      -129 (-113)     -49 ( -38)     *CASH      -36  \par ( -38)\par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070817e38h0006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
