\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dollar slumps to 5-mth lows on credit woes   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2007\par 06:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar fell over 3 percent on Thursday to its lowest in nearly five months against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors dumped riskier assets and piled into safe-haven government debt amid a global credit squeeze.   \par \par \uc2 The Aussie <AUD=> briefly fell as low as $0.7998, its weakest since March 21 and a loss of nearly 11 percent since its July 25, 18-year peak of $0.8871, as contagion from the deepening credit market woes led to sharp falls in regional stock markets.   \par \par \uc2 It recovered to $0.8051/56 at 4:00 p.m. (0600 GMT), but was still down from $0.8246/49 late on Wednesday here. Carry trade liquidation also saw the Aussie fall to 92.66 yen <AUDJPY=R>, down over 16 percent since its July 20, 16-year high of 107.72 yen. It recovered to 93.53/63 in late trade, down from 96.10/20 on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Under carry trades investors borrow in the low-yielding Japanese yen to buy high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Aussie and New Zealand dollars, and these transactions had driven the Aussie's gains earlier in the year.   \par \par \uc2 "The Aussie has literally dropped like a stone," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 Westpac.\par \par \uc2 "There's no liquidity or logic to it, and certainly no mercy. In fact, this could well be described as a disruptive market requiring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in a smooth things out."   \par \par \uc2 The sheer magnitude of the Australian dollar's fall, following on from heavy losses the previous day, led to expectations that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check its slide.   \par \par \uc2 The RBA has been routinely selling Australian dollars in the last few years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose. The last time it bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a net basis in a month was back in late 2001.   \par \par \uc2 All eyes are now on a testimony by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Glenn Stevens at a parliamentary hearing on Friday for his latest thoughts on monetary policy and the economy in light of the ongoing turmoil in global markets.   \par \par \uc2 Australian Treasurer Peter Costello said on Thursday that although there had been a significant fallout from the credit woes in the United States, the Australian banking sector was well capitalised and had sufficient liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 The Australian share market <.AXJO> ended 1.3 percent lower, recovering well after being down over 5 percent at one point on Thursday, with financial shares leading the way down.   \par \par \uc2 Australian mortgage lender RAMS Home Loans Group Ltd. said it had failed to roll over A$6.17 billion ($5.1 billion) in maturing notes due to a lack of liquidity in the U.S. commercial paper market, causing its shares to dive 36 percent.   \par \par \uc2 As investors fled risk, many sought the relative safety of sovereign debt, sharply lifting Australian bonds futures. The 10-year bond contract <YTCc1> added 0.02 points to 94.11, lowering yields to 5.89 percent when just a month ago they had been up around 6.30 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Three-year bond futures <YTTc1> jumped 0.06 points to 93.800, tracking huge gains in short-term U.S. Treasuries as some investors speculated that the turmoil in markets would force the Federal Reserve to cut interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 That, in turn, saw bill futures price in less risk the RBA would follow last week's interest rate with another, at least for the next few months.   \par \par \uc2 "Our forecasts have for some time incorporated another 25 basis point rate rise by the RBA early next year," said Stephen Halmarick, co-head economic and market analysis at  \uc2 Citigroup.\par \par \uc2 "However, if borrowing costs continue to rise relative to the official cash rate and global growth forecasts are marked down in coming weeks, then no further rate rise would become our central case."    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------(Snapshot at 4:30 p.m./0630\par GMT)---------------- FUTURES                                  \par CASH YIELD      90-DAY BILL<YBAc1> (SEP) 93.250(+0.020)\par <AU3MBB=RR> 6.86 (6.95) 3-YR BOND  <YTTc1> (SEP) 93.800(+0.060)\par <AU3YT=RR>  6.21 (6.27) 10-YR BOND <YTCc1> (SEP) 94.110(+0.020)\par <AU10YT=RR> 5.88 (5.90) AUD/USD <AUD=> 0.8045 (0.8262) US 10-YR\par <US10YT=RR> 4.70 (4.70)\par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par AUD VS   2-YR           10-YR           *AUD 3-YR/10-YR SPREAD\par USD      +198 (+199)    +118 (+120)     *FUTURES   -31.0\par (-35.0) CAD      +197 (+207)    +145 (+151)     *AUD 2-YR/10-YR\par SPREAD NZD      -113 (-123)     -38 ( -46)     *CASH      -38  \par ( -42)\par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070819e38g00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
