\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-High Aussie dlr keeps inflation at bay ... for now   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Morrissey   \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2007\par 06:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, July 24 (Reuters) - A surge in the Australian dollar to an 18-year high is keeping a lid on import prices, lessening the chance of near-term rises in interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  Strong domestic demand and a tight labour market could fuel inflation, but analysts cite the Aussie's strength as a text book case of how a floating exchange rate can help insulate an economy from upward price pressures.   \par \par \uc2  "By crimping growth and working to reduce import prices, the Aussie's uptrend increasingly is limiting inflation pressures and weakening the case for any near-term RBA tightening," said Rory Robertson, interest rate strategist at  \uc2 Macquarie Bank.\par \par \uc2  The litmus test is on Wednesday with the release of the quarterly consumer inflation report -- a key number that guides the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) in its rate decisions.   \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll forecasts second-quarter headline consumer prices rising 1 percent, while the annual pace should slow to 1.9 percent from 2.4 percent in the March quarter. [ID:nSYA22609].   \par \par \uc2  "The strong Aussie has had some impact on inflation,"  \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie said. "The last couple of quarters of inflation have been very well behaved."   \par \par \uc2  Known six years ago as the "Pacific Peso" and the "Little Aussie Bleeder," the Aussie has made a remarkable turnaround.   \par \par \uc2  A combination of firm commodity prices, demand from booming China for Australia's minerals, a strong outlook for global growth, domestic economic reforms, Australia's interest rate differential, and a frail U.S. dollar have all helped the Aussie.   \par \par \uc2  The Aussie rose to $0.8863 on Tuesday, its highest level since February 1989 and a gain of 12 percent so far this year. It was trading at below $0.4800 as recently as 2001.   \par \par \uc2  NO WORRIES   \par \par \uc2  The commodities boom, in particular, has justified the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains in the eyes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . RBA Governor Glenn Stevens last week said he was not surprised by its strength, which the market took as a sign the bank was in no hurry to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  That stands in stark contrast with \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Pacific neighbour New Zealand, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times to stem an export-damaging rise in the kiwi <NZD=>.   \par \par \uc2  The RBA's equanimity reflects the role a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plays in keeping down import prices, important for a country where imports equal almost a quarter of the economy.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, domestic data showed only a slight 0.1 percent rise in import prices in the second-quarter even including a hefty 12.8 percent jump in fuel costs. Most other import prices fell, dragged down by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The Aussie also helped to hold back producer prices in the second quarter. Annual growth in prices slowed to a three-year low of 2.3 percent.   \par \par \uc2  "While stronger domestic prices are a concern, lower yearly inflation gives the RBA more time to evaluate the impact of activity data on the broader inflation outlook," said Joshua Williamson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at TD Securities.   \par \par \uc2     TEMPORARY REPRIEVE   \par \par \uc2  There are caveats.   \par \par \uc2  Stevens has noted strong demand for services eventually puts more pressure on prices and wages and that the rise of the Aussie is a temporary reprieve.   \par \par \uc2  "Nor can we assume that a rising exchange rate will exert a consistent dampening force on inflation of traded goods and services over the longer term, since exchange rates do not keep rising indefinitely," Stevens told a conference last month.   \par \par \uc2  Inflation is likely to rise in 2008, he warned, leading the financial markets to price in an eventual rate rise.   \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect the RBA to keep rates at 6.25 percent in August for the ninth straight month. The 30-day interbank futures market <0#YIB:> has priced a 60 percent chance of a November rate rise, down from 80 percent two weeks ago. That risk moves up to 74 percent in December.   \par \par \uc2  "(The) strong Australian economy suggests that a rate hike over the next six to 12 months can't be ruled out," said John Kyriakopoulis, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at nabCapital.      \par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA-ECONOMY/DOLLAR (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|ND   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070727e37o000f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
