\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie inches towards 18-yr peak vs USD, bonds dip   \b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2007\par 00:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---------------(Snapshot at 8:05 a.m./2205 GMT)----------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (vs Late Sydney)      DEBT FUTURES (Night Session) <AUD=>\par  0.8470 (0.8455)  90-DAY <YBAc1> (JUN) 93.42  (93.41)\par <AUDEUR=R> 0.6324 (0.6310)  3-YR   <YTTc1> (JUN) 93.540(93.550)\par <AUDJPY=R> 104.77 (104.55)  10-YR  <YTCc1> (JUN) 93.710(93.730)\par <AUDNZD=R> 1.1088 (1.1068)  US 10-YR <US10YT=RR> 5.19  (5.15) \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par AU$S/Term range *Support *Restance *RSI-14 *MA-10  *MA-20     \par   *0.8270/8490  *0.8300  *0.8500   *66.529  *0.8428 *0.8373   \par ------------------------(June 22)------------------------------\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * Australian dollar offshore range roughly $0.8430/75.   \f28 \par \par \uc2  * The Australian dollar <AUD=> hovered a shade below its 18-year peak of $0.8477 against the U.S. dollar in offshore trade, supported by increased demand for carry trades.   \par \par \uc2  * The Aussie rose to a fresh 16-year peak against the Japanese yen <AUDJPY=>, reaching as high as 104.79 yen as investors continued to choose assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher returns.   \par \par \uc2  * In carry trades, investors borrow in the low-interest bearing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Japanese yen to buy higher-yielding assets or \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Aussie or the New Zealand kiwi.   \par \par \uc2  * The New Zealand dollar <NZD=> was off a 22-year post float high but above $0.7600, as investors chasing its yield shrugged off any threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  * Although \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept interest rates unchanged since December last year, financial markets are pricing in a rate hike by October as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to check inflationary pressures in a robustly growing economy.   \par \par \uc2  * The yen <JPY=> was mired near 4-\u189\'00\'BD   year lows against the U.S. dollar on a view that Japanese interest rates are unlikely to rise in a hurry.   \par \par \uc2  * Against the euro <EUR=>, the U.S. dollar was little changed, giving up earlier gains made in the wake of a strong reading in the Philadelphia area factory output report.   \par \par \uc2  * Reuters/Jefferies CRB Index <.CRB> was down 0.47 percent.   \par \par \uc2  * Industrial metal prices were mixed at the London Metal Exchange, with copper slipping but lead and nickel both gaining ground.   \par \par \uc2  * On the New York Mercantile Exchange, U.S. crude for August delivery <CLQ7> settled down 21 cents or 0.3 percent at $68.65 a barrel, trading from $68.28 to $69.88.   \par \par \uc2  * Spot gold <XAU=> was quoted at $651.20/652.70 in early Asian trade, after sliding to $648.20/649.70 an ounce in late New York trade from $654.50/656.0 in late Wednesday trade.   \par \par \uc2  * Aussie bond futures fell, tracking long-dated U.S. Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2  * Three-year Aussie bond futures <YTTc1> fell 0.01 points to 93.54 while the 10-year bond contract <YTCc1> dropped 0.025 points to 93.71.      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-DOLLAR/BONDS|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070624e36l000k1\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\c